OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Nonuniqueness-with-local-conservation-for-the-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-October-5-2026", "title": "Nonuniqueness with local conservation for the hard-sphere Boltzmann equation", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Nonuniqueness-with-local-conservation-for-the-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-October-5-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "363", "family_title": "Nonuniqueness with local conservation for the hard-sphere Boltzmann equation", "abstract_html": "We prove nonuniqueness for the three-dimensional periodic hard-sphere Boltzmann equation among global entropy solutions satisfying exact local conservation of mass, momentum, and kinetic energy. We construct one nonnegative initial density with bounded velocity support and finite mass, energy, and absolute entropy that gives rise to two distinct such solutions. Both are strongly continuous in <i>L</i><sup>1</sup>, and their collision gain and loss terms are integrable with every polynomial velocity weight on every bounded time interval.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exist \\(R<\\infty\\) and a nonnegative datum \\(F_0\\) such that \\( \\operatorname{supp}_vF_0\\subset B_R(0),\\qquad \\int_\\Omega F_0(1+|v|^2+|\\log F_0|)\\,dx\\,dv<\\infty, \\) for which \\( D_tF=Q(F,F) \\) has two global …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is Theorem \\(1.2\\) of the paper.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathbb T^3=\\mathbb R^3/\\mathbb Z^3\\) have volume one, let \\(\\Omega=\\mathbb T_x^3\\times\\mathbb R_v^3\\), and write \\(D_t=\\partial_t+v\\cdot\\nabla_x\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(v,v_*\\in\\mathbb R^3\\) and \\(\\omega\\in S^2\\), set\n  \\[\n  a=(v-v_*)\\cdot\\omega,\\qquad v'=v-a\\omega,\\qquad v_*'=v_*+a\\omega.\n  \\]\n  The sphere carries ordinary surface measure, and\n  \\[\n  Q^+(F,F)(v)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3\\times S^2}|a|F(v')F(v_*')\\,dv_*\\,d\\omega,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  Q^-(F,F)(v)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3\\times S^2}|a|F(v)F(v_*)\\,dv_*\\,d\\omega,\n  \\qquad Q=Q^+-Q^-.\n  \\]\n  Every factor has the same time and position.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(M(v)=(2\\pi)^{-3/2}e^{-|v|^2/2}\\), \\(G=F/M\\), and\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal H_M(F)=\\int_\\Omega\\bigl(F\\log(F/M)-F+M\\bigr)\\,dx\\,dv,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal D(F)=\\frac14\\int_{\\mathbb T^3\\times\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R^3\\times S^2}\n  |a|(F'F_*'-FF_*)\\log\\frac{F'F_*'}{FF_*}\\,dx\\,dv\\,dv_*\\,d\\omega.\n  \\]\n  The dissipation integrand takes its nonnegative lower-semicontinuous extended value at zero, and \\(0|\\log0|=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">“Entropy solution with local conservation” means Definition \\(1.1\\) of the paper:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. \\(F\\ge0\\), \\(F\\in C_w([0,\\infty);L^1(\\Omega))\\), \\(F(0)=F_0\\), with continuity against every bounded measurable test, and, for every finite \\(T\\),\n     \\[\n     \\sup_{0\\le t\\le T}\\int_\\Omega F(t)(1+|v|^2+|\\log F(t)|)\\,dx\\,dv&lt;\\infty.\n     \\]</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. \\(Q^\\pm(F,F)/(1+G)\\in L^1([0,T]\\times\\Omega)\\). For every \\(\\beta\\in C^1([0,\\infty))\\) with \\(\\beta(0)=0\\), \\(|\\beta'(z)|\\le C_\\beta/(1+z)\\), and every smooth \\(\\varphi\\), periodic in \\(x\\) and compactly supported in \\([0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R_v^3\\),\n     \\[\n     \\int_0^\\infty\\!\\!\\int_\\Omega\n     \\bigl[M\\beta(G)D_t\\varphi+\\beta'(G)Q(F,F)\\varphi\\bigr]\\,dx\\,dv\\,dt\n     +\\int_\\Omega M\\beta(F_0/M)\\varphi(0)\\,dx\\,dv=0.\n     \\]</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">3. Total momentum is conserved and, at every \\(t\\ge0\\),\n     \\[\n     \\int_\\Omega |v|^2F(t)\\le\\int_\\Omega |v|^2F_0,\\qquad\n     \\mathcal H_M(F(t))+\\int_0^t\\mathcal D(F(s))\\,ds\\le\\mathcal H_M(F_0).\n     \\]</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">4. For every finite \\(T\\), \\(\\int_0^T\\int_\\Omega(1+|v|^3)F&lt;\\infty\\). For every\n     \\(\\psi\\in\\{1,v_1,v_2,v_3,|v|^2/2\\}\\) and \\(\\phi\\in C_c^\\infty([0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb T^3)\\),\n     \\[\n     \\int_0^\\infty\\!\\!\\int_\\Omega F\\psi D_t\\phi\\,dx\\,dv\\,dt\n     +\\int_\\Omega F_0\\psi\\phi(0,x)\\,dx\\,dv=0.\n     \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exist \\(R&lt;\\infty\\) and a nonnegative datum \\(F_0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\operatorname{supp}_vF_0\\subset B_R(0),\\qquad\n\\int_\\Omega F_0(1+|v|^2+|\\log F_0|)\\,dx\\,dv&lt;\\infty,\n\\]\nfor which\n\\[\nD_tF=Q(F,F)\n\\]\nhas two global entropy solutions with local conservation, differing on a set of positive \\((t,x,v)\\)-measure. Both belong to \\(C([0,\\infty);L^1(\\Omega))\\). Moreover, for every finite \\(T\\) and every \\(k\\ge0\\),\n\\[\n\\int_0^T\\!\\!\\int_\\Omega\n\\langle v\\rangle^k\\bigl[Q^+(F,F)+Q^-(F,F)\\bigr]\\,dx\\,dv\\,dt&lt;\\infty,\n\\qquad \\langle v\\rangle=(1+|v|^2)^{1/2}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the datum \\(F_0\\) from Proposition \\(2\\), whose bounds and compact velocity support were proved there. Fix parameters in the order required by Propositions \\(9\\) and \\(5\\). Proposition \\(6\\) supplies a dormant global limit \\(F^d\\), and, for every \\(T\\in(0,T_0]\\), global limits of cap approximations from Proposition \\(4\\). Every such limit has datum \\(F_0\\), exact local conservation, strong \\(L^1\\) continuity, entropy admissibility, and all polynomially weighted collision integrals.</p><p>Suppose there are horizons \\(T_k\\downarrow0\\) at which a cap limit equals \\(F^d\\) almost everywhere on \\((0,T_k)\\). For each fixed \\(T_k\\), use the subsequence converging to that common limit. Let \\(K_j\\) be a compact exhaustion of \\(\\mathcal V\\), and choose nonnegative smooth compact \\(\\zeta_j\\ge1\\) on \\(K_j\\). Select finite indices \\(N_k\\) diagonally so that \\(s_{N_k}/T_k&lt;1/k\\) and normalized approximation-limit pairings against \\(\\zeta_1,\\ldots,\\zeta_k\\) differ by less than \\(1/k\\).</p><p>At each fixed horizon, these become fixed smooth physical tests; periodize their spatial factors to use Proposition \\(6\\)'s weak convergence. Proposition \\(10\\) makes the common-limit pairings tend to zero. Nonnegativity then gives\n\\[\nf_k(t,x,v):=\\frac{F_k(T_kt,T_kx,v)}{T_km_{T_k}}\n\\longrightarrow0\n\\quad\\text{in local }L^1(\\mathcal V).\n\\]\nNo convergence rate uniform in \\(T_k\\) is used.</p><p>Choose cap times \\(t_{*,k}\\) and put \\(r_k=t_{*,k}+s_{N_k}\\). Proposition \\(7\\) gives, after subselection,\n\\[\ns_{N_k}/r_k\\to l,\\qquad T_k/r_k\\to U\\in[1,\\infty],\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{h_k(r_kt,r_kx,v)}{m_{T_k}r_k}\\rightharpoonup g\n\\]\nlocally on \\(0&lt;t&lt;U\\). Proposition \\(8\\) gives \\(g\\ne0\\); Proposition \\(9\\) gives \\(U&lt;\\infty\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\rho_k=r_k/T_k\\to\\rho=1/U&gt;0.\n\\]\nSince \\(F_k=c_k+u_k+h_k\\ge h_k\\) through the cap horizon,\n\\[\n0\\le\\frac{h_k(r_kt,r_kx,v)}{m_{T_k}r_k}\n\\le\\rho_k^{-1}f_k(\\rho_kt,\\rho_kx,v).\n\\]</p><p>For a compact \\(K\\subset(0,U)\\times\\mathbb R_x^3\\times\\{\\bar v\\ne0\\}\\), all scaled images lie in one compact \\(K'\\Subset\\mathcal V\\) for large \\(k\\). The time-space Jacobian is \\(\\rho_k^4\\), so\n\\[\n\\int_K\\frac{h_k(r_kt,r_kx,v)}{m_{T_k}r_k}\\,dt\\,dx\\,dv\n\\le\\rho_k^{-5}\\int_{K'}f_k\\,dt\\,dx\\,dv\\to0.\n\\]\nThus \\(g=0\\) off the velocity axis. The cap \\(g\\le(t+l)H\\) makes \\(g\\) a density, and the axis is null, so \\(g=0\\) almost everywhere, contradicting Proposition \\(8\\).</p><p>Therefore, for all sufficiently small horizons no cap limit can equal the fixed dormant limit on the corresponding early interval. Choose one such cap limit. It and \\(F^d\\) are distinct global solutions with all asserted properties and differ on a set of positive spacetime-velocity measure. Rotate back from \\(\\mathbb R^3/\\Lambda\\) to \\(\\mathbb T^3\\); the equation, entropy properties, and local conservation laws are invariant under the simultaneous rotation. This proves the theorem.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The gain is symmetric.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This proposition collects the collision estimates of Section \\(3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(v=(\\bar v,v_3)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\times\\mathbb R\\), fix \\(1&lt;q&lt;2\\), and put\n  \\[\n  W(v)=1+|\\bar v|^{-q},\\qquad\n  H(v)=e^{-|v|^2}\\langle v\\rangle^{-m}W(v),\\qquad m=100.\n  \\]\n  Values on the velocity axis are irrelevant. The function \\(H\\) has every polynomial moment and belongs to \\(L^p(\\mathbb R^3)\\) for \\(1\\le p&lt;2/q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  P(f,g)(v)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3\\times S^2}\n  |(v-v_*)\\cdot\\omega|f(v')g(v_*')\\,dv_*\\,d\\omega.\n  \\]\n  Its cutoff version \\(P^b\\), \\(b\\ge1\\), uses the additional factor\n  \\[\n  \\min\\{1,b/|v-v_*|\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal G_d\\) be the transverse Gaussian probability with density\n  \\((\\pi d^2)^{-1}e^{-|\\bar p|^2/d^2}\\), with \\(\\mathcal G_0=\\delta_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Following Definition \\(3.3\\), an elementary sheet in \\(\\mathcal S_L\\), \\(L\\ge1\\), is\n  \\[\n  c(dp)=\\mathcal G_d(d\\bar p)\\rho(dp_3),\\qquad\n  0\\le d\\le\\tfrac18,\\qquad \\int_{\\mathbb R}e^{8a^2}\\rho(da)\\le L.\n  \\]\n  Probability mixtures of elementary sheets are allowed. Sheet domination with amplitude \\(A_0\\) means domination by \\(A_0\\) times such a mixture.</p><p class=\"li\">For a sheet and \\(\\bar v\\ne0\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal K_c(v;dp\\,d\\xi)\n  =2c(dp)\\delta((v-p)\\cdot(\\xi-v))\\frac{H(\\xi)}{H(v)}\\,d\\xi.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The gain is symmetric. It extends to the velocity measures used below, satisfies uniform sheet and quadratic weighted bounds, has the tail and axial-slice controls established in Lemmas \\(1.1\\)–\\(1.6\\), and satisfies the small-mass estimate\n\\[\n0\\le j\\le BH\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nP(H,j)(v)\\le H(v)\\left(\\varepsilon+C_{B,\\varepsilon}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}j(p)\\,dp\\right)\n\\]\nfor every \\(B&lt;\\infty\\), \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), and almost every \\(v\\). All upper bounds hold uniformly for \\(P^b\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma \\(1.1\\) fixes the exact normalization and symmetry. Lemma \\(1.2\\) supplies the geometric averaging estimates. Lemmas \\(1.3\\)–\\(1.5\\) give uniform normalized sheet bounds, velocity tightness, large-output decay, and parameter-tail control, including compressed axial laws. Lemma \\(1.6\\) gives the quadratic bound, and Lemma \\(1.7\\) gives the stated small-mass estimate. Every upper estimate is preserved by the cutoff factor.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The symmetric gain has the exact Carleman formula \\( P(c,h)(v)=2\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}c(dp)\\int_{\\mathbb R^3} \\delta((v-p)\\cdot(\\xi-v))h(\\xi)\\,d\\xi. \\) For \\(p\\ne v\\), the inner delta denotes plane surface measure divided …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(3.1\\), with the normalization and collision law of Theorem \\(1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Inputs are nonnegative densities; the first input may also be a nonnegative velocity measure.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The symmetric gain has the exact Carleman formula\n\\[\nP(c,h)(v)=2\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}c(dp)\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\n\\delta((v-p)\\cdot(\\xi-v))h(\\xi)\\,d\\xi.\n\\]\nFor \\(p\\ne v\\), the inner delta denotes plane surface measure divided by \\(|v-p|\\). For incoming velocities \\(p,\\xi\\), the outgoing measure has total mass \\(2\\pi|p-\\xi|\\) and is uniform on the sphere with diameter \\([p,\\xi]\\). The formula defines the measure-input gain whenever it is finite almost everywhere.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(g=p-\\xi\\). Reflection sends \\(g\\) to \\(g-2(g\\cdot\\omega)\\omega\\). On either hemisphere, \\(|g\\cdot\\omega|\\,d\\omega\\) pushes forward to \\((|g|/4)\\,d\\sigma\\). Both hemispheres therefore give the one-output measure\n\\[\n\\frac{|p-\\xi|}{2}\\int_{S^2}\n\\psi\\left(\\frac{p+\\xi}{2}+\\frac{|p-\\xi|}{2}\\sigma\\right)\\,d\\sigma.\n\\]\nIts mass is \\(2\\pi|p-\\xi|\\), and its density on the image sphere is \\(2/|p-\\xi|\\).</p><p>That sphere is the zero set of \\(f(v)=(v-p)\\cdot(\\xi-v)\\), and on it\n\\(|\\nabla_vf|=|p-\\xi|\\). Coarea identifies the outgoing measure with\n\\(2\\delta(f(v))\\,dv\\). Integrating against the incoming inputs and using Tonelli gives the formula. The usual elastic substitution identifies this expression with the stated gain. The expression is symmetric in the two inputs. Coincident incoming velocities have zero collision rate.</p><p>For the cutoff, insert \\(\\min(1,b/|p-\\xi|)\\). Incoming and outgoing relative speeds agree, so collision symmetries are preserved, and the factor is at most one.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\frac1{W(v)}\\int c(dp)\\int_{|\\xi-v|\\le R} \\delta((v-p)\\cdot(\\xi-v))W(\\xi)\\,d\\xi\\le C_qR^2, \\) and …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(3.2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal U_d\\) be uniform probability on \\(\\{|\\bar p|&lt;d\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\lambda\\) be any probability mixture of the measures \\(\\mathcal U_d\\), \\(d&gt;0\\), and \\(\\delta_0\\), let \\(\\rho\\) be any axial probability, and set \\(c=\\lambda\\otimes\\rho\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(R\\ge1\\), \\(\\bar v\\ne0\\), and let \\(I\\subset\\mathbb R\\) have length \\(0&lt;\\ell\\le2R\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\frac1{W(v)}\\int c(dp)\\int_{|\\xi-v|\\le R}\n\\delta((v-p)\\cdot(\\xi-v))W(\\xi)\\,d\\xi\\le C_qR^2,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\frac1{W(v)}\\int c(dp)\n\\int_{\\substack{|\\xi-v|\\le R\\\\ \\xi_3\\in I}}\n\\delta((v-p)\\cdot(\\xi-v))W(\\xi)\\,d\\xi\n\\le C_q(R\\ell+R^{q-1}\\ell^{2-q}).\n\\]\nConstants are independent of transverse widths and of \\(\\rho\\). In particular these hold for \\(\\lambda=\\mathcal G_d\\), \\(d\\ge0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Set \\(r=|\\bar v|\\), \\(n=\\bar v-\\bar p\\), \\(e_n=n/|n|\\). For fixed \\(p_3,\\xi_3\\), transverse delta integration is line measure divided by \\(|n|\\), on a line at signed distance\n\\[\nA-B(\\xi_3-v_3),\\qquad\nA=e_n\\cdot\\bar v,\\qquad B=(v_3-p_3)/|n|.\n\\]\nThe regular part is at most \\(2R/|n|\\). The singular part is bounded by\n\\[\n\\frac{a_q}{|n|}|A-B(\\xi_3-v_3)|^{1-q},\\qquad\na_q=\\int_{\\mathbb R}(1+s^2)^{-q/2}\\,ds&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nFor every interval \\(J\\subset[-R,R]\\) of length at most \\(\\ell\\),\n\\[\n\\int_J|A-Bx|^{1-q}\\,dx\n\\le C_qR^{q-1}\\ell^{2-q}|A|^{1-q}.\n\\]\nIf \\(2R|B|\\le|A|\\), use the lower bound \\(|A-Bx|\\ge|A|/2\\). Otherwise integrate about the possible root and use \\(1-q&lt;0\\).</p><p>Next,\n\\[\n\\int\\frac{1+|e_n\\cdot e_{\\bar v}|^{1-q}}{|n|}\\,\\lambda(d\\bar p)\n\\le \\frac{C_q}{r}.\n\\]\nFor a disk of radius \\(d\\le r/2\\), both required lower bounds are immediate. If \\(d&gt;r/2\\), polar coordinates for \\(n\\) cancel the divisor \\(|n|^{-1}\\); the angular integral\n\\(\\int_0^{2\\pi}(1+|\\cos\\theta|^{1-q})\\,d\\theta\\) is finite since \\(q&lt;2\\). Dividing by disk area gives \\(C_q/d\\le2C_q/r\\). The atom at zero is direct. The Gaussian is a probability mixture of disks with radius-mixture density\n\\[\n2a^3d^{-4}e^{-a^2/d^2}\\,da.\n\\]</p><p>Combining these estimates bounds the sliced expression by\n\\[\nC_qR\\ell\\frac{r^{-1}}{1+r^{-q}}\n+C_qR^{q-1}\\ell^{2-q}\\frac{r^{-q}}{1+r^{-q}}.\n\\]\nBoth fractions are bounded. The estimate is independent of \\(p_3\\), so integration against \\(\\rho\\) preserves it. Taking the whole axial range of length \\(2R\\) gives the first estimate.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( P(c,H)(v)\\le C_{q,m,L}H(v) \\) almost everywhere.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(3.4\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(c\\in\\mathcal S_L\\), with \\(L&lt;\\infty\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nP(c,H)(v)\\le C_{q,m,L}H(v)\n\\]\nalmost everywhere. More quantitatively, for \\(R\\ge1\\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_c(v;R&lt;|p|+|\\xi-v|\\le2R)\n\\le C_{q,m,L}R^2e^{-cR^2},\n\\]\nwhere \\(c&gt;0\\) is absolute. Under sheet domination with amplitude \\(A_0\\), multiply these bounds by \\(A_0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>It suffices to treat an elementary sheet. Write \\(k=\\xi-v\\), \\(w=p+k\\). On the collision plane,\n\\[\n|p|^2+|\\xi|^2=|v|^2+|w|^2,\\qquad\n\\frac{H(\\xi)}{H(v)}\n=e^{|p|^2-|w|^2}\\frac{\\langle v\\rangle^m}{\\langle\\xi\\rangle^m}\n\\frac{W(\\xi)}{W(v)}.\n\\]\nThe same identity implies \\(\\langle v\\rangle\\le\\langle p\\rangle\\langle\\xi\\rangle\\). Since\n\\(\\langle p\\rangle^m\\le C_me^{|p|^2}\\), the kernel is bounded by\n\\[\nC_me^{2|p|^2}c(dp)e^{-|w|^2}\n\\delta((v-p)\\cdot k)\\frac{W(v+k)}{W(v)}\\,dk.\n\\]\nFor \\(0&lt;d\\le1/8\\),\n\\[\ne^{2|\\bar p|^2}\\mathcal G_d(d\\bar p)\n\\le4e^{-46|\\bar p|^2}\\mathcal G_{2d}(d\\bar p).\n\\]\nThe tilted axial measure \\(\\widetilde\\rho(da)=e^{2a^2}\\rho(da)\\) has mass at most \\(L\\) and\n\\[\n\\widetilde\\rho\\{|a|&gt;u\\}\\le Le^{-6u^2}.\n\\]\nThus the tilted cold measure is dominated by \\(4L\\) times a product of a transverse Gaussian and an arbitrary axial probability. Its restriction to \\(|p|&gt;u\\) is dominated by \\(CLe^{-cu^2}\\) times at most two such product probabilities, obtained by splitting transverse and axial tails.</p><p>On \\(R&lt;|p|+|k|\\le2R\\), either \\(|p|&gt;R/4\\) or \\(|w|&gt;R/2\\), since\n\\(|p|+|k|\\le2|p|+|w|\\). The first alternative supplies a tilted-measure Gaussian tail; the second supplies \\(e^{-R^2/4}\\). The remaining geometric integral, with \\(|k|\\le2R\\), is bounded by Lemma \\(1.2\\). This proves the shell estimate. The base region is bounded by the same lemma and the tilted mass. Summing dyadic shells proves the gain bound and finiteness of the normalized measure.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Uniformly in sheets and outgoing velocities off the axis, \\( \\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\sup_{c\\in\\mathcal S_L}\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_v \\mathcal K_c(v;|p|+|\\xi-v|>R)=0, \\) and …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(3.5\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(L&lt;\\infty\\), and let \\(c\\in\\mathcal S_L\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Uniformly in sheets and outgoing velocities off the axis,\n\\[\n\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\sup_{c\\in\\mathcal S_L}\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_v\n\\mathcal K_c(v;|p|+|\\xi-v|&gt;R)=0,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\lim_{M\\to\\infty}\\sup_{c\\in\\mathcal S_L}\n\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_{|v|&gt;M}\n\\mathcal K_c(v;\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R^3)=0.\n\\]\nBoth hold under uniformly bounded sheet amplitudes.</p>", "proof": "<p>The shell estimate in Lemma \\(1.3\\) proves the first assertion. Retain\n\\(|p|+|\\xi-v|\\le R\\). The exponential and polynomial factors in the weight ratio are bounded by a constant depending on \\(R\\).</p><p>If \\(|\\bar v|&gt;2R+1\\), then \\(|\\bar v-\\bar p|\\ge|\\bar v|/2\\) and\n\\(|\\bar\\xi|\\ge|\\bar v|-R\\). Transverse line integration followed by axial integration gives \\(C_R/|\\bar v|\\). If \\(|v_3|&gt;2R+1\\), integrate the delta in \\(\\xi_3\\), with divisor at least \\(|v_3|/2\\), and use\n\\[\n\\sup_{a\\in\\mathbb R^2}\\int_{|\\zeta-a|\\le R}(1+|\\zeta|^{-q})\\,d\\zeta\n\\le C_q(R^2+R^{2-q}).\n\\]\nThis gives \\(C_R/|v_3|\\). At least one output component becomes large with \\(|v|\\). Remove the retained-region restriction using the first assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The image measure is dominated by \\(C_0\\) times a sheet in \\(\\mathcal S_{L_A}\\), where \\(L_A\\) depends only on \\(A\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(3.6\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A parameter measure has density\n  \\[\n  0\\le j(w)\\le C_0\\pi^{-3/2}e^{-|w|^2},\n  \\qquad w=(\\bar w,w_3)\\in\\mathbb R^3.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Its velocity image is\n  \\[\n  \\bar p=d\\bar w,\\qquad p_3=\\Phi(w_3),\\qquad\n  0\\le d\\le\\tfrac1{16},\\qquad\n  |\\Phi(a)|\\le A+\\delta|a|,\\quad0\\le\\delta\\le\\tfrac1{16},\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\Phi\\) is measurable.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The image measure is dominated by \\(C_0\\) times a sheet in \\(\\mathcal S_{L_A}\\), where \\(L_A\\) depends only on \\(A\\). Its gain satisfies Lemma \\(1.3\\). If \\(c_{&gt;B}\\) is the image of\n\\(\\mathbf1_{\\{|w|&gt;B\\}}j(w)\\,dw\\), then\n\\[\n\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_v\\frac{P(c_{&gt;B},H)(v)}{H(v)}\n\\le C_{q,m,A,C_0}e^{-B^2/2}.\n\\]\nProbability mixtures preserve the assertions. A positive mixing measure of mass \\(B_0&lt;\\infty\\) multiplies domination amplitudes and upper bounds by \\(B_0\\), without changing \\(L_A\\). Measurable masks in \\([0,1]\\) preserve all assertions.</p>", "proof": "<p>The Gaussian majorant is a product. Its transverse image is \\(\\mathcal G_d\\), and its axial image is\n\\(\\rho=\\Phi_*(\\pi^{-1/2}e^{-a^2}\\,da)\\); no inversion or differentiation of \\(\\Phi\\) is needed. Since\n\\[\ne^{8|\\Phi(a)|^2}\\le e^{16A^2}e^{16\\delta^2a^2},\n\\]\neven the wider probability \\((2\\pi)^{-1/2}e^{-a^2/2}\\,da\\) has the required exponential moment, bounded by \\(2e^{16A^2}\\).</p><p>On the parameter tail,\n\\[\n\\mathbf1_{\\{|w|&gt;B\\}}e^{-|w|^2}\n\\le e^{-B^2/2}e^{-|w|^2/2}.\n\\]\nAfter normalization, the right side is \\(2^{3/2}e^{-B^2/2}\\) times a Gaussian probability with widths enlarged by \\(\\sqrt2\\). Its transverse width is still below \\(1/8\\), and its axial image has the preceding moment. Apply Lemma \\(1.3\\).</p><p>Mix the dominating sheets for probability mixtures. For a mixing measure of positive mass, normalize it and retain the mass as amplitude. The zero-mass case is immediate. The same argument applies to component parameter tails. Masks only decrease nonnegative measures.</p><p>For the compressed profile\n\\[\np_3=\\upsilon(z-tp_3)+dw_3,\\qquad u=\\upsilon(z-tu),\n\\]\nwith \\(\\upsilon\\) nondecreasing, the map \\(p\\mapsto p-\\upsilon(z-tp)\\) has inverse Lipschitz constant at most one, so \\(|p_3-u|\\le d|w_3|\\). Thus the lemma applies whenever \\(|u|\\le A\\). Fixed Gaussian exponents other than one are handled by rescaling parameters and decreasing physical width bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.6", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( P(H,H)\\le C_{q,m}H \\) almost everywhere.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(3.7\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the weight \\(H\\) from Proposition \\(1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nP(H,H)\\le C_{q,m}H\n\\]\nalmost everywhere. The proof works for every \\(m&gt;5\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The integrand is symmetric in \\(p,\\xi\\). On the collision plane,\n\\(|v|^2\\le|p|^2+|\\xi|^2\\), so, at a factor of two, retain \\(|\\xi|\\ge|v|/2\\). The weight identity gives\n\\[\n\\frac{P(H,H)(v)}{H(v)}\n\\le C_m\\int_{|\\xi|\\ge|v|/2}\n\\delta((v-p)\\cdot(\\xi-v))e^{-|p+\\xi-v|^2}\n\\langle p\\rangle^{-m}\\frac{W(p)W(\\xi)}{W(v)}\\,dp\\,d\\xi.\n\\]\nWrite \\(k=\\xi-v\\), \\(w=p+k\\). On \\(R&lt;|p|+|k|\\le2R\\), either \\(|p|&gt;R/4\\) or \\(|w|&gt;R/2\\), and\n\\[\n\\langle p\\rangle^{-m}e^{-|w|^2}\n\\le C_mR^{-m}e^{-|w|^2/2}.\n\\]</p><p>The measure \\(W(p)\\mathbf1_{\\{|p|\\le2R\\}}\\,dp\\) is dominated by a product whose transverse factor is a positive mixture of centered disks. Indeed,\n\\[\n|\\bar p|^{-q}\\mathbf1_{\\{|\\bar p|\\le D\\}}\n=D^{-q}\\mathbf1_{\\{|\\bar p|\\le D\\}}\n+q\\int_0^D a^{-q-1}\\mathbf1_{\\{|\\bar p|\\le a\\}}\\,da.\n\\]\nThe resulting product mixture has total mass at most \\(C_qR^3\\). Discard the remaining Gaussian and apply Lemma \\(1.2\\), with \\(|k|\\le2R\\), to each normalized product. The shell is bounded by \\(C_{q,m}R^{5-m}\\). The base region is bounded by the same argument with \\(R=1\\). Dyadic summation proves the assertion for \\(m&gt;5\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.7", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is \\(C_{B,\\varepsilon}<\\infty\\) such that \\( P(H,j)(v)\\le H(v) \\left(\\varepsilon+C_{B,\\varepsilon}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}j(p)\\,dp\\right) \\) almost everywhere.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(3.8\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(B&lt;\\infty\\), \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), and \\(j\\) be measurable with \\(0\\le j\\le BH\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(C_{B,\\varepsilon}&lt;\\infty\\) such that\n\\[\nP(H,j)(v)\\le H(v)\n\\left(\\varepsilon+C_{B,\\varepsilon}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}j(p)\\,dp\\right)\n\\]\nalmost everywhere. The same constant works for \\(P^b\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The case \\(B=0\\) is immediate. First establish\n\\[\n\\lim_{M\\to\\infty}\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_{|v|&gt;M}\n\\frac{P(H,H)(v)}{H(v)}=0.\n\\]\nUse the symmetric reduction and shell estimate from Lemma \\(1.6\\) to remove\n\\(|p|+|\\xi-v|&gt;R\\). On the retained region the remaining integrand is bounded by\n\\[\nC_R W(p)\\delta((v-p)\\cdot(\\xi-v))\\frac{W(\\xi)}{W(v)}.\n\\]\nFor large \\(|\\bar v|\\), transverse line integration gives \\(C_R/|\\bar v|\\). For large \\(|v_3|\\), integrating in \\(\\xi_3\\) gives \\(C_R/|v_3|\\). Here\n\\(\\int_{|p|\\le R}W(p)\\,dp&lt;\\infty\\). First let \\(|v|\\to\\infty\\), then \\(R\\to\\infty\\).</p><p>Choose \\(M\\) so that \\(BP(H,H)\\le\\varepsilon H\\) on \\(|v|&gt;M\\). On \\(|v|\\le M\\), remove large inputs \\(|p|+|\\xi|&gt;L\\), using the symmetric shell deletion. On retained inputs the normalized quadratic integrand is bounded by\n\\[\nC_{M,L}\\delta((v-p)\\cdot(\\xi-v))\\frac{W(p)W(\\xi)}{W(v)}.\n\\]\nFor \\(|\\bar p|&lt;\\delta\\), the disk-mixture argument and Lemma \\(1.2\\) give cost at most\n\\(C_{M,L}(\\delta^2+\\delta^{2-q})\\). Symmetry gives the same deletion for\n\\(|\\bar\\xi|&lt;\\delta\\). The domination \\(j\\le BH\\) transfers all deletions to the mixed gain.</p><p>Thus, for\n\\[\nK=\\{|p|\\le L,\\ |\\bar p|\\ge\\delta\\},\\qquad H_K=H\\mathbf1_K,\\quad j_K=j\\mathbf1_K,\n\\]\nthe deleted contribution is at most \\(\\varepsilon H/2\\). The function \\(H_K\\) is bounded and compactly supported. By symmetry and the plane formula,\n\\[\nP(H_K,j_K)(v)\\le C_{L,\\delta}\\int_K\\frac{j_K(p)}{|v-p|}\\,dp.\n\\]\nThe part \\(|v-p|&lt;\\rho\\) is at most \\(C_{B,L,\\delta}\\rho^2\\), since \\(j_K\\) is bounded. The complement is at most\n\\(C_{L,\\delta}\\rho^{-1}\\int j\\). On \\(|v|\\le M\\), \\(H\\) has a positive lower bound off its null axis. Choose \\(\\rho\\) to make the near contribution at most \\(\\varepsilon H/2\\), and absorb fixed factors into \\(C_{B,\\varepsilon}\\). Finally \\(P^b\\le P\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The datum has bounded velocity support and finite mass, energy, and absolute entropy.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This proposition collects Section \\(4\\), including paper Proposition \\(4.3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Rotate space and velocity together using the orthonormal frame\n  \\[\n  \\frac{(-\\sqrt2,1,0)}{\\sqrt3},\\quad(0,0,1),\\quad\n  \\frac{(1,\\sqrt2,0)}{\\sqrt3}.\n  \\]\n  The period lattice becomes a rotation \\(\\Lambda\\) of \\(\\mathbb Z^3\\). Write\n  \\(x=(y,z)\\), \\(v=(\\bar v,v_3)\\). Spatial dilations are taken in the lift.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix\n  \\[\n  K\\ge1,\\quad0&lt;\\eta\\le\\log2,\\quad0&lt;\\alpha\\le\\tfrac14,\\quad0&lt;\\gamma\\le1.\n  \\]\n  Choose \\(J\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)\\), \\(J\\ge0\\), supported in the unit ball, with integral one. Set\n  \\[\n  s_j=s_0e^{-j\\eta},\\quad p_j=s_j^2,\\quad\n  c_\\eta=\\frac{e^\\eta-1}{16},\\quad d_s=d_0s^{10},\\quad\n  S(r)=\\operatorname{sech}^2r,\n  \\]\n  where \\(s_0\\le1/8\\) and \\(d_0&gt;0\\) is sufficiently small. Put\n  \\[\n  a_s^\\sigma(z)=\\sigma+\\alpha\\tanh(\\gamma z/s),\\quad\n  a=\\tfrac18,\\quad b_{\\mathrm{sp}}=\\tfrac1{16}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E_j^\\sigma\\) be the annulus\n  \\[\n  s_j+c_\\eta p_j&lt;|y|&lt;e^\\eta s_j-c_\\eta p_j\n  \\]\n  intersected with stripes\n  \\[\n  y_1/p_j\\pmod1\\in\n  \\begin{cases}\n  (0,1/4),&amp;\\sigma=+1,\\\\\n  (1/2,3/4),&amp;\\sigma=-1.\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(i=(j,\\sigma,n)\\), \\(n\\in\\Lambda\\), and \\((y_c,z_c)=x-n\\), define\n  \\[\n  c_i^0(x,v)=\\mathbf1_{E_j^\\sigma}(y_c)\\mathbf1_{(-a,a)}(z_c)\n  \\frac Ks S(\\gamma z_c/s)d_s^{-3}\n  J\\left(\\frac{v-a_s^\\sigma(z_c)e_3}{d_s}\\right),\\quad s=s_j.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a sufficiently large numerical \\(\\beta_0\\) and put \\(\\mu(v)=e^{-\\beta_0|v|^2}\\). Let \\(D_{\\mathrm{hole}}\\) be the open \\(2\\delta\\)-neighborhood on the torus of the axial segment \\([-1/3,1/3]\\), with \\(0&lt;\\delta&lt;1/32\\). Define\n  \\[\n  u^0=\\mathbf1_{D_{\\mathrm{hole}}^c}\\mu\\mathbf1_{\\{|v|\\le R_\\mu\\}},\n  \\qquad F_0=u^0+\\sum_i c_i^0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose nonzero \\(\\chi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R_x^3\\times\\mathbb R_v^3)\\), \\(\\chi\\ge0\\), and \\(L_0\\|\\chi\\|_H&gt;1\\). For \\(j\\le N\\), the seed is zero or\n  \\[\n  h_{N,T,\\lambda}^0(x,v)\n  =\\lambda m_Ts_N\\sum_{n\\in\\Lambda}\\chi((x-n)/s_N,v),\n  \\quad m_T=T^p,\\quad0\\le\\lambda\\le L_0.\n  \\]\n  Initially \\(p&gt;0\\), \\(0&lt;T\\le t_1\\le1/32\\); their required choices are made in Proposition \\(4\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  \\nu_f^b(v)=2\\pi\\int\\min\\{b,|v-p|\\}f(p)\\,dp,\\qquad b=b_N\\to\\infty.\n  \\]\n  Until \\(t_1\\), with \\(c=\\sum_i c_i\\), \\(F=c+u+h\\), solve\n  \\[\n  D_tc_i+\\nu_F^bc_i=P^b(c_i,c_i),\\qquad\n  D_tu+\\nu_F^bu=P^b(u,u),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  D_th+\\nu_F^bh=P^b(h,h)+2P^b(c+u,h)+\\mathcal S,\n  \\quad\n  \\mathcal S=2P^b(c,u)+\\sum_{i\\ne i'}P^b(c_i,c_{i'}).\n  \\]\n  The cross-color sum is ordered.</p><p class=\"li\">At \\(t_1\\), replace \\(u+h\\) by \\(w\\). Each scale remains active until\n  \\(L_s=s^{-1/2}\\). At \\(L_s\\), add its colors to \\(w\\). Between transfers,\n  \\[\n  F=c+w,\\qquad\n  D_tw+\\nu_F^bw=P^b(w,w)+2P^b(c,w)+\\sum_{i\\ne i'}P^b(c_i,c_{i'}).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The datum has bounded velocity support and finite mass, energy, and absolute entropy. Its finite truncations, with the allowed seeds, converge to \\(F_0\\) in every polynomially weighted \\(L^1\\) and in relative entropy with respect to \\(M\\) and \\(\\mu\\). Their initial \\(\\mu\\)-relative entropy can be made arbitrarily small uniformly in the approximation and seed parameters.</p><p>For each fixed approximation the colored system has a unique nonnegative local mild solution with bounded total \\(H\\)-norm; it continues while this norm remains bounded and depends continuously on its initial data on common bounded intervals. The total density solves the cutoff Boltzmann equation across transfers, conserves the collision invariants locally and globally, and obeys the cutoff entropy inequality.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma \\(2.2\\) proves the datum and approximation assertions; Lemma \\(2.3\\) proves the finite evolution assertions.</p><p>Record the entropy consequence used later. Define\n\\[\nD_F(t,x)=\\int\\langle v\\rangle|F(t,x,v)-\\mu(v)|\\,dv,\\qquad\nD_-(t,x)=\\int\\langle v\\rangle(\\mu-F)_+\\,dv.\n\\]\nFor any sufficiently small prescribed \\(\\varepsilon_E&gt;0\\), parameters can be chosen so that, on every existing approximation interval,\n\\[\n\\|D_F(t)\\|_{L_x^1}\\le\\varepsilon_E,\\qquad\n0\\le D_-(t,x)\\le\\int\\langle v\\rangle\\mu(v)\\,dv.\n\\]\nIndeed, \\((\\sqrt z-1)^2\\le z\\log z-z+1\\) and Cauchy–Schwarz give\n\\[\n\\|D_F(t)\\|_1\n\\le\\mathcal H_\\mu(F(t))^{1/2}\n\\left(2\\int\\langle v\\rangle^2(F(t)+\\mu)\\,dx\\,dv\\right)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nThe second factor is uniformly bounded by conserved mass and energy; the first is small by the entropy inequality. No pointwise smallness of \\(D_F\\) is asserted here. Lemma \\(2.1\\) supplies the lattice separation needed for the long-time cold estimates.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a numerical \\(c>0\\) such that \\( |\\bar n|\\ge\\frac{c}{1+|n|}\\qquad(n\\in\\Lambda\\setminus\\{0\\}). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(4.1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Lambda\\) be the rotated lattice in Proposition \\(2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a numerical \\(c&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\n|\\bar n|\\ge\\frac{c}{1+|n|}\\qquad(n\\in\\Lambda\\setminus\\{0\\}).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>An original integer vector \\((k,m,\\ell)\\) has transverse coordinates\n\\(((m-\\sqrt2k)/\\sqrt3,\\ell)\\). If \\(\\ell\\ne0\\), its transverse length is at least one. Otherwise\n\\[\n|m-\\sqrt2k|\n=\\frac{|m^2-2k^2|}{|m+\\sqrt2k|}\n\\ge\\frac1{C(1+|k|+|m|)}.\n\\]\nThe numerator is a nonzero integer. Rotation preserves length.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The datum is nonnegative, has bounded velocity support, and \\( \\int F_0(1+|v|^2+|\\log F_0|)\\,dx\\,dv<\\infty. \\) Its truncations converge in every polynomially weighted \\(L^1\\) and in \\(\\mathcal H_M,\\mathcal H_\\mu\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(4.2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the datum and seeded truncations in Proposition \\(2\\), with \\(s_0\\) sufficiently small compared with \\(\\delta\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a positive Maxwellian \\(\\rho\\), write\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal H_\\rho(F)=\\int\\bigl(F\\log(F/\\rho)-F+\\rho\\bigr)\\,dx\\,dv.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The datum is nonnegative, has bounded velocity support, and\n\\[\n\\int F_0(1+|v|^2+|\\log F_0|)\\,dx\\,dv&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nIts truncations converge in every polynomially weighted \\(L^1\\) and in \\(\\mathcal H_M,\\mathcal H_\\mu\\), uniformly for \\(0&lt;T\\le t_1\\), \\(0\\le\\lambda\\le L_0\\). Initial \\(\\mathcal H_\\mu\\) is arbitrarily small by first decreasing \\(\\delta\\) and increasing \\(R_\\mu\\), then decreasing \\(s_0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For small \\(s_0\\), jets lie in the hole. Their supports are disjoint: annuli and stripe patterns are disjoint in one copy, and prototype supports lie in a ball of radius less than \\(1/2\\), so distinct lattice copies cannot overlap.</p><p>Integration over one period of a lattice sum equals integration of a prototype over \\(\\mathbb R_x^3\\). Its transverse area is \\(O(s^2)\\), while\n\\[\n\\int_{-a}^a S(\\gamma z/s)\\,dz\\le2s/\\gamma.\n\\]\nThe velocity factor integrates to one and has support in \\(\\{|v|&lt;2\\}\\). Each sign prototype therefore has every fixed velocity moment \\(O(s^2)\\). On its positive set, the logarithm splits into\n\\[\n\\log((K/s)d_s^{-3}),\\qquad \\log S(\\gamma z/s),\\qquad \\log J(w).\n\\]\nThe first is \\(O(1+|\\log s|)\\), and\n\\(\\int S|\\log S|,\\int J|\\log J|&lt;\\infty\\). Absolute entropy is\n\\(O(s^2(1+|\\log s|))\\), summable over geometric scales.</p><p>For the seed \\(b=h_{N,T,\\lambda}^0\\),\n\\[\n\\int b\\langle v\\rangle^k\\le C_ks_N^4,\\qquad\n\\int b|\\log b|\\le Cs_N^4(1+|\\log s_N|).\n\\]\nThe existing truncated density \\(f\\) obeys \\(f\\le Cs_N^{-31}\\), and\n\\[\n|(f+b)\\log(f+b)-f\\log f|\n\\le b\\{2+\\log^+(f+b)\\}+b|\\log b|.\n\\]\nThis follows by integrating \\(1+\\log(f+r)\\) over \\(0&lt;r&lt;b\\), bounding its positive logarithm by its terminal value and using\n\\(\\int_0^b\\log^-(f+r)\\,dr\\le b(1+|\\log b|)\\). Thus the seed entropy change tends to zero uniformly.</p><p>Removing the hole and velocity tail from \\(\\mu\\) costs precisely the removed mass in \\(\\mathcal H_\\mu\\). Jet and seed contributions are bounded by the preceding absolute entropy, mass, and energy sums, which tend to zero with \\(s_0\\). This proves the prescribed order of choices.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a unique nonnegative local mild solution with bounded total \\(H\\)-norm.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Proposition \\(4.3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(N,b,T,\\lambda\\) and the colored equations and transfer schedule in Proposition \\(2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the norm of the pointwise sum of absolute component values, divided by \\(H\\), for the colored system.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a unique nonnegative local mild solution with bounded total \\(H\\)-norm. It continues while that norm remains bounded, depends continuously on its initial data on every common bounded interval, and each component norm is continuous between transfers.</p><p>On such intervals, mass, momentum, and energy are conserved, exact local invariant balances hold with all polynomial fluxes integrable, and, for \\(\\rho=M,\\mu\\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_\\rho(F(t))+\\int_0^t\\mathcal D^b(F(s))\\,ds\n\\le\\mathcal H_\\rho(F(0))\n\\]\nat every time of existence. Here \\(\\mathcal D^b\\) uses the same cutoff.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition \\(1\\) and\n\\[\n\\|\\nu_f^b\\|_\\infty\\le2\\pi b\\|f\\|_H\\int H\n\\]\nmake gain and loss locally Lipschitz in the colored norm. Initially disjoint copies have finite total norm. Picard iteration in free coordinates gives local existence, the loss integrating factor and positive gain iteration give nonnegativity, and repetition gives continuation and continuous dependence. Periodic covariance is preserved by uniqueness. No norm continuity of translation for measurable data is needed: increments along flights are Lipschitz in the norm, and free transport is isometric. Transfers regroup finitely many scales and preserve \\(F\\).</p><p>The weight has every polynomial moment and belongs to \\(L^{1+\\epsilon}\\) for some \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\). Consequently cutoff gains and losses are integrable with every polynomial weight. Integrate the path formula and use particle interchange and the elastic substitution to obtain global invariants and the corresponding local tested balances.</p><p>For entropy, put \\(g(v)=e^{-2|v|^2}\\), \\(F_\\epsilon=F+\\epsilon g\\). Along flights the derivative of\n\\(F_\\epsilon\\log(F_\\epsilon/\\rho)-F_\\epsilon+\\rho\\) is\n\\(Q^b(F,F)\\log(F_\\epsilon/\\rho)\\). Replacing \\(Q^b(F,F)\\) by\n\\(Q^b(F_\\epsilon,F_\\epsilon)\\) causes an error tending to zero: the operator difference is \\(O(\\epsilon)H\\), and\n\\[\n|\\log(F_\\epsilon/\\rho)|\n\\le C(1+|\\log\\epsilon|+|v|^2+\\log^+(1/|\\bar v|)).\n\\]\nThe resulting integrated error is \\(O(\\epsilon(1+|\\log\\epsilon|))\\). For the shifted density, symmetry gives minus the cutoff dissipation; the logarithm of the Maxwellian reference is a collision invariant. Dominated convergence passes regularized entropies, and Fatou passes dissipation. The path formula includes every section, so identities hold at every time, including transfers.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For sufficiently small \\(s_0\\), active enlargements are disjoint, and \\( 0\\le c_i\\le\\mathcal U_i:=f_i+\\epsilon_sm_i,\\qquad \\epsilon_s=C_*L_sd_s/s. \\) If \\(\\nu_F^b\\ge\\kappa>0\\) on an interval beginning at \\(t_1\\), then …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This proposition collects Section \\(5\\), including paper Proposition \\(5.3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the finite colored evolutions, fixed jet parameters, and scales of Proposition \\(2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(i=(j,\\sigma,n)\\), set\n  \\[\n  \\widehat E_j^\\sigma\n  =\\{y:\\operatorname{dist}(y,E_j^\\sigma)\\le(c_\\eta/4)s_j^2\\},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  O_i(t)=n+\\widehat E_j^\\sigma\n  \\times[-a-2t-b_{\\mathrm{sp}},a+2t+b_{\\mathrm{sp}}].\n  \\]\n  A label is active while \\(t\\le L_{s_i}\\). At \\((t,x)\\), the main label is the unique active label with \\(x\\in O_i(t)\\), if there is one; all other labels are nonmain. Let \\(O(t)\\) be the union projected onto the torus.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U_{s,\\sigma}(t,z)\\) solve\n  \\[\n  U_{s,\\sigma}=a_s^\\sigma(z-tU_{s,\\sigma}).\n  \\]\n  For a fixed label put \\(s=s_j\\), \\(L=L_s\\), \\(U=U_{s,\\sigma}(t,z_c)\\),\n  \\[\n  \\tau=z_c-tU,\\quad \\xi=v-Ue_3,\\quad\n  D_y=\\operatorname{dist}(y_c,E_j^\\sigma),\\quad\n  D_z=\\operatorname{dist}(\\tau,[-a,a]).\n  \\]\n  Define\n  \\[\n  f_i(t,x,v)=c_i^0(x-tv,v),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  m_i=C_m\\frac Ks d_s^{-3}e^{t/L}\n  \\exp\\left[-\\frac{(2-t/L)|\\xi|^2}{d_s^2}\n  -\\frac{D_y^2+D_z^2}{8d_s^2Lt}\\right],\n  \\]\n  with its path trace at zero.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently small \\(s_0\\), active enlargements are disjoint, and\n\\[\n0\\le c_i\\le\\mathcal U_i:=f_i+\\epsilon_sm_i,\\qquad\n\\epsilon_s=C_*L_sd_s/s.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\nu_F^b\\ge\\kappa&gt;0\\) on an interval beginning at \\(t_1\\), then\n\\[\nc_i(t)\\le e^{-\\kappa(t-t_1)}\\mathcal U_i(t).\n\\]\nMain-color velocity measures have sheet amplitude \\(C/(t+s)\\), nonmain colors have a uniformly small Gaussian sum, retirement norms grow at most polynomially in \\(1/s\\), and\n\\[\n|O(t)|\\le Cs_0^{3/2}.\n\\]\nAll constants are independent of approximation indices and of the hot density.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas \\(3.1\\)–\\(3.3\\) prove disjointness and the individual comparisons. Lemma \\(3.4\\) gives the velocity-measure bounds; Lemmas \\(3.5\\) and \\(3.6\\) give nonmain summability, retirement bounds, and tube volume.</p><p>All assertions are essential estimates. Free coordinates preserve measure, so Fubini supplies almost-everywhere path inequalities and full-measure endpoint sets at every prescribed time. Countably many approximation indices, labels, and interval choices can be handled simultaneously.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For sufficiently small \\(s_0\\), \\( O_i(t)\\cap O_{i'}(t)=\\varnothing \\) for distinct labels whenever \\(t\\le\\min(L_{s_i},L_{s_{i'}})\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(5.1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use active enlargements from Proposition \\(3\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently small \\(s_0\\),\n\\[\nO_i(t)\\cap O_{i'}(t)=\\varnothing\n\\]\nfor distinct labels whenever \\(t\\le\\min(L_{s_i},L_{s_{i'}})\\). Also\n\\[\n\\widehat E_j^\\sigma\\subset\\{s_j&lt;|y|&lt;e^\\eta s_j\\}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Within a copy, radial margins exceed enlargement radii. Adjacent radial cores have gap\n\\(c_\\eta(s_j^2+s_{j+1}^2)\\), while enlargement radii sum to one quarter of this. Opposite stripe patterns have gap \\(s_j^2/4\\), larger than \\(c_\\eta s_j^2/2\\).</p><p>For distinct copies, put \\(S_*=\\max(s_i,s_{i'})\\). Intersection would imply\n\\[\n|\\bar n-\\bar n'|\\le4S_*,\\qquad\n|n-n'|\\le C(1+t)\\le C(1+S_*^{-1/2}).\n\\]\nLemma \\(2.1\\) gives \\(|\\bar n-\\bar n'|\\ge c'S_*^{1/2}\\), contradicting the first inequality for uniformly small \\(s_0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "A fixed sufficiently large \\(C_m\\) gives \\( f_i\\le m_i,\\qquad D_tm_i\\ge\\frac1{L_s}\\left(1+\\frac78\\frac{|\\xi|^2}{d_s^2}\\right)m_i, \\) and \\( P(m_i,m_i)=m_i\\nu_{m_i} \\le C\\frac Ks(d_s+|\\xi|)m_i. \\) The differential …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(5.2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(f_i,m_i,\\xi\\) from Proposition \\(3\\), for \\(0\\le t\\le L_s\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A fixed sufficiently large \\(C_m\\) gives\n\\[\nf_i\\le m_i,\\qquad\nD_tm_i\\ge\\frac1{L_s}\\left(1+\\frac78\\frac{|\\xi|^2}{d_s^2}\\right)m_i,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nP(m_i,m_i)=m_i\\nu_{m_i}\n\\le C\\frac Ks(d_s+|\\xi|)m_i.\n\\]\nThe differential inequality holds almost everywhere along flights. For small \\(s_0\\), \\(f_i&gt;0\\) implies \\(x\\in O_i(t)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(A=(a_s^\\sigma)'(\\tau)\\ge0\\). Implicit differentiation gives\n\\[\nU_z=\\frac A{1+tA}\\ge0,\\quad U_t=-UU_z,\\quad\nD_tU=(v_3-U)U_z,\\quad\nD_t\\tau=\\frac{v_3-U}{1+tA}.\n\\]\nThus \\(D_t|\\xi|^2=-2U_z\\xi_3^2\\),\n\\(|D_tD_y|\\le|\\bar\\xi|\\), and \\(|D_tD_z|\\le|\\xi_3|\\). With \\(r=(D_y,D_z)\\),\n\\[\nD_t\\log m_i\n=\\frac1L+\\frac{|\\xi|^2}{Ld_s^2}\n+\\frac{2(2-t/L)U_z\\xi_3^2}{d_s^2}\n+\\frac{|r|^2}{8d_s^2Lt^2}\n-\\frac{r\\cdot D_tr}{4d_s^2Lt}.\n\\]\nThe last two terms are at least \\(-|D_tr|^2/(8d_s^2L)\\). Since\n\\(|D_tr|\\le|\\xi|\\), the differential estimate follows; the large axial derivative has favorable sign.</p><p>On a free-core flight, \\(v=a_s^\\sigma(z_c^0)e_3+d_sw\\), \\(|w|\\le1\\). Monotonicity of\n\\(p\\mapsto p-a_s^\\sigma(z_c-tp)\\) gives\n\\[\n|\\xi|\\le d_s,\\qquad D_y\\le td_s|\\bar w|,\\qquad D_z\\le td_s|w_3|.\n\\]\nThe distance exponent is at most \\(t/(8L)\\), and the velocity exponent at most two; choose \\(C_m\\) using \\(\\|J\\|_\\infty\\). Since\n\\(L_sd_s=d_0s^{19/2}\\ll s^2\\), transverse displacement stays within the enlargement. Axial speed is below two.</p><p>At fixed \\((t,x)\\), \\(m_i\\) is a scalar Maxwellian centered at \\(Ue_3\\). Detailed balance gives \\(P(m_i,m_i)=m_i\\nu_{m_i}\\). Its mass is at most \\(CK/s\\), and its first moment about \\(Ue_3\\) at most \\(CKd_s/s\\), giving the estimate.</p><p>Along a path starting on the closed initial support, distances vanish initially and are locally Lipschitz, so squared-distance-over-time terms tend to zero. Away from the support, the barrier tends to zero. These give its trace and justify integration from zero without differentiating masks.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every active color obeys \\( 0\\le c_i(t,x,v)\\le\\mathcal U_i(t,x,v),\\qquad0\\le t\\le L_{s_i}. \\) If additionally \\(\\nu_F^b\\ge\\kappa>0\\) on an interval beginning at \\(t_1\\), then …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Proposition \\(5.3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\epsilon_s=C_*L_sd_s/s\\), \\(\\mathcal U_i=f_i+\\epsilon_sm_i\\), and take \\(s_0\\) sufficiently small.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every active color obeys\n\\[\n0\\le c_i(t,x,v)\\le\\mathcal U_i(t,x,v),\\qquad0\\le t\\le L_{s_i}.\n\\]\nIf additionally \\(\\nu_F^b\\ge\\kappa&gt;0\\) on an interval beginning at \\(t_1\\), then\n\\[\nc_i(t,x,v)\\le e^{-\\kappa(t-t_1)}\\mathcal U_i(t,x,v)\n\\]\nthere, through retirement.</p>", "proof": "<p>Reduce \\(s_0\\) so that \\(\\epsilon_s\\le1\\). Then \\(\\mathcal U_i\\le2m_i\\). Lemma \\(3.2\\) gives\n\\[\nP^b(\\mathcal U_i,\\mathcal U_i)\n\\le4C(K/s)(d_s+|\\xi|)m_i,\n\\]\nwhereas\n\\[\nD_t\\mathcal U_i\n\\ge\\frac{\\epsilon_s}{L_s}\n\\left(1+\\frac78|\\xi|^2/d_s^2\\right)m_i.\n\\]\nSince \\(1+z^2\\ge c(1+z)\\), choose \\(C_*\\) so that\n\\(D_t\\mathcal U_i\\ge P^b(\\mathcal U_i,\\mathcal U_i)\\). Drop the nonnegative loss and compare positive gains from the initial trace.</p><p>At a fixed approximation, \\(m_i\\le C_sH\\), and\n\\(z_i=(c_i-\\mathcal U_i)_+\\) has upper source\n\\(2P^b(\\mathcal U_i,z_i)+P^b(z_i,z_i)\\) from zero. The bilinear norm estimate and Gronwall give \\(z_i=0\\). The trace in Lemma \\(3.2\\) permits starting from positive time and passing to zero.</p><p>Finally \\(e^{-\\kappa(t-t_1)}\\mathcal U_i\\) is a supersolution for \\(D_t+\\kappa\\): the derivative has one exponential factor and the quadratic gain two. Compare from \\(t_1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The measure \\(f_i(t,x,p)\\,dp\\) is the pushforward of \\( \\frac{KS(\\gamma(z_c-tp_3)/s)} {s+t\\alpha\\gamma S(\\gamma(z_c-tp_3)/s)}J(w)\\,dw, \\) with initial masks evaluated at \\(x-tp\\), under …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(5.4\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\((t,x)\\), an active label of scale \\(s\\), and \\(t\\le L_s\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The measure \\(f_i(t,x,p)\\,dp\\) is the pushforward of\n\\[\n\\frac{KS(\\gamma(z_c-tp_3)/s)}\n{s+t\\alpha\\gamma S(\\gamma(z_c-tp_3)/s)}J(w)\\,dw,\n\\]\nwith initial masks evaluated at \\(x-tp\\), under\n\\[\n\\bar p=d_s\\bar w,\\qquad p_3=a_s^\\sigma(z_c-tp_3)+d_sw_3.\n\\]\nIt has sheet amplitude \\(C/(t+s)\\). The remainder has sheet amplitude at most\n\\[\nCL_sd_s/s^2\\le Cs^{15/2}\\le C/(t+s).\n\\]\nThus \\(\\mathcal U_i\\,dp\\) has amplitude \\(C/(t+s)\\). For every \\(k\\ge0\\), a main color satisfies\n\\[\n\\int\\langle p\\rangle^kc_i\\,dp\\le\\frac{C_k}{t+s},\\qquad\n\\int_{|p|&gt;R}\\langle p\\rangle^kc_i\\,dp\n\\le\\frac{C_ke^{-c_kR^2}}{t+s},\\quad R\\ge3.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The transverse Jacobian is \\(d_s^2\\), and\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial p_3}{\\partial w_3}\n=\\frac{d_s}{1+t(a_s^\\sigma)'(z_c-tp_3)}.\n\\]\nMultiplying by the initial density gives the formula. Its coefficient is bounded by \\(C/(s+t)\\), since \\(S\\le1\\) and \\(\\alpha\\gamma&gt;0\\). A product Gaussian dominates \\(J\\), and monotonicity gives\n\\(|p_3-U|\\le d_s|w_3|\\). Lemma \\(1.5\\) gives uniform sheet domination despite axial compression.</p><p>The remainder is a Gaussian centered at \\(Ue_3\\), of width comparable with \\(d_s\\), with mass at most \\(C\\epsilon_sK/s=CL_sd_s/s^2\\). Its sheet moment is uniform because \\(|U|\\le1+\\alpha\\). Gaussian moments and tails, together with Lemma \\(3.3\\), prove the assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is \\(\\theta_0(s_0)\\to0\\) as \\(s_0\\downarrow0\\) such that \\( \\sum_{\\substack{i\\ \\mathrm{active}\\\\x\\notin O_i(t)}}\\mathcal U_i(t,x,v) \\le\\theta_0(s_0)e^{-4|v|^2}. \\) For \\(0<t\\le1\\), the coefficient may instead be …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(5.5\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Sum any subset of active nonmain labels.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(\\theta_0(s_0)\\to0\\) as \\(s_0\\downarrow0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{i\\ \\mathrm{active}\\\\x\\notin O_i(t)}}\\mathcal U_i(t,x,v)\n\\le\\theta_0(s_0)e^{-4|v|^2}.\n\\]\nFor \\(0&lt;t\\le1\\), the coefficient may instead be \\(C_{A,B}t^As_0^B\\), for any fixed \\(A,B&gt;0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Free cores vanish off their enlargements. For a nonmain label either\n\\(D_y\\ge(c_\\eta/4)s^2\\), or \\(|z_c|&gt;a+2t+b_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\), which gives\n\\(D_z\\ge b_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\). Completing the velocity square gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal U_i\\le Cs^{-31}e^{-4|v|^2}\n\\exp\\left[-\\frac{D_y^2+D_z^2}{8d_s^2L_st}\\right].\n\\]\nHalf the distance exponential is at most\n\\(\\exp[-c/(s^{16}L_st)]\\), hence at most \\(e^{-c/s^{15}}\\) since \\(t\\le L_s\\). It absorbs every polynomial in \\(1/s\\), and for \\(t\\le1\\) arbitrary powers of \\(t\\) and \\(s\\).</p><p>For the remaining lattice sum,\n\\[\nD_y\\ge(|y_c|-C)_+,\\qquad\nD_z\\ge(|z_c|-C(1+t))_+.\n\\]\nThe Gaussian distance scale \\(d_s\\sqrt{L_st}\\) is bounded, and the central region has diameter \\(O(1+L_s)\\). Unit lattice separation gives at most \\(C(R+1)^3\\) points in radius \\(R\\). Summation loses only a fixed polynomial in \\(1/s\\), absorbed by the extracted exponential. Sum geometric scales.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.6", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For fixed constants \\(C,C'>0\\), \\( \\left\\|\\sum_{i:s_i=s}\\mathcal U_i(L_s)\\right\\|_H\\le Cs^{-C'}. \\) Moreover, \\( |O(t)|\\le\\sum_{j\\ge1}Cs_j^2(1+L_{s_j})\\le Cs_0^{3/2}. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(5.6\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use all active labels and the retirement times \\(L_s=s^{-1/2}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For fixed constants \\(C,C'&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\left\\|\\sum_{i:s_i=s}\\mathcal U_i(L_s)\\right\\|_H\\le Cs^{-C'}.\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n|O(t)|\\le\\sum_{j\\ge1}Cs_j^2(1+L_{s_j})\\le Cs_0^{3/2}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Use \\(f_i\\le m_i\\) and the Gaussian lattice sum from Lemma \\(3.5\\), without extracting a small distance factor. Prefactors and the number of central copies are polynomial in \\(1/s\\). Completing the velocity square leaves \\(Ce^{-4|v|^2}\\le CH\\).</p><p>The transverse area at scale \\(s_j\\) is \\(O(s_j^2)\\), and active axial length is \\(O(1+L_{s_j})\\). Projection does not increase volume. Sum\n\\(C\\sum_j(s_j^2+s_j^{3/2})\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For fixed \\(\\delta>0\\), sufficiently small \\(s_0\\), and \\(0<T\\le T_0\\), every finite approximation admits \\(\\lambda\\in(0,L_0)\\) such that its hot component exists on \\([0,T]\\) and …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This proposition collects Section \\(6\\), including paper Propositions \\(6.3\\) and \\(6.5\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the construction and finite solutions of Propositions \\(2\\)–\\(3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D_0(x)=\\operatorname{dist}(x,D_{\\mathrm{hole}}^c)\\), and write \\(Z(t)=\\|h(t)\\|_H\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A dormant solution has zero hot seed.</p><p class=\"li\">The fixed time \\(t_1&gt;0\\), independent of \\(\\delta,R_\\mu,s_0\\), is chosen in Lemma \\(4.1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose\n  \\[\n  p&gt;C_2+1,\\qquad A&gt;p+C_2+3,\\qquad m_T=T^p,\n  \\]\n  where \\(C_2\\) is the uniform coefficient in Lemma \\(4.2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For fixed \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), sufficiently small \\(s_0\\), and \\(0&lt;T\\le T_0\\), every finite approximation admits \\(\\lambda\\in(0,L_0)\\) such that its hot component exists on \\([0,T]\\) and\n\\[\nZ(t)\\le m_T(t+s_N),\\qquad\n\\max_{0\\le t\\le T}\\frac{Z(t)}{m_T(t+s_N)}=1.\n\\]\nDormant solutions have \\(Z(t)\\le C_{A,\\delta}t^A\\) on a common initial interval.</p><p>For every prescribed \\(\\zeta\\in(0,1)\\), \\(s_0,T_0\\) may be chosen so that all dormant and selected solutions continue to \\(t_1\\) and\n\\[\n\\sup_{0\\le t\\le t_1}\\|h(t)\\|_H\\le\\zeta,\n\\]\nuniformly in \\(N,b,R_\\mu,T\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas \\(4.1\\)–\\(4.2\\) give bath and source controls independently of hot bounds. Lemma \\(4.3\\) selects a cap-attaining seed and constructs dormant solutions. Lemma \\(4.4\\) supplies the additional spatial smallness needed away from zero, and Lemma \\(4.5\\) gives uniform continuation and prescribed hot smallness through \\(t_1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are \\(t_1\\in(0,1/32]\\), \\(\\beta'>4\\), and \\(C,c>0\\), independent of \\(\\delta,R_\\mu,s_0\\), such that \\( 0\\le u(t,x,v)\\le Ce^{-\\beta'|v|^2} \\exp[-cD_0(x)^2/t],\\qquad0<t\\le t_1. \\) For sufficiently small \\(s_0\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(6.1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the bath equation in Proposition \\(2\\), without assuming a hot bound.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(t_1\\in(0,1/32]\\), \\(\\beta'&gt;4\\), and \\(C,c&gt;0\\), independent of \\(\\delta,R_\\mu,s_0\\), such that\n\\[\n0\\le u(t,x,v)\\le Ce^{-\\beta'|v|^2}\n\\exp[-cD_0(x)^2/t],\\qquad0&lt;t\\le t_1.\n\\]\nFor sufficiently small \\(s_0\\) depending on \\(\\delta\\),\n\\[\nD_0(x)\\ge\\delta\\qquad(0\\le t\\le t_1,\\ x\\in O(t)).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The distance \\(D_0\\) is \\(1\\)-Lipschitz. Fix \\(4&lt;\\beta'&lt;\\beta_0\\), and consider\n\\[\nB(t,x,v)=e^{C_1t}\n\\exp[-(\\beta_0-C_1t)|v|^2-cD_0(x)^2/t].\n\\]\nAlong almost every flight,\n\\[\nD_t\\log B\n=C_1+C_1|v|^2+cD_0^2/t^2-(2cD_0/t)v\\cdot\\nabla D_0\n\\ge C_1+(C_1-c)|v|^2.\n\\]\nAt fixed \\((t,x)\\), \\(B\\) is Maxwellian in velocity, so\n\\(P(B,B)=B\\nu_B\\le C(1+|v|)B\\) while\n\\(\\beta_0-C_1t\\ge\\beta'&gt;4\\) and \\(e^{C_1t}\\le2\\). Choose \\(C_1\\) large, then \\(t_1\\) small.</p><p>Its path trace dominates \\(u^0\\): when \\(D_0(x)=0\\),\n\\(D_0(x+tv)\\le t|v|\\), and otherwise the trace is zero, as is \\(u^0\\).\nPositive-gain comparison with loss omitted gives \\(u\\le B\\), without differentiating the initial mask.</p><p>For \\(t\\le1/32\\), enlargement axial half-length is at most\n\\[\n1/8+1/16+1/16=1/4&lt;1/3.\n\\]\nIts transverse distance from the defining segment is at most \\(s_0\\), hence\n\\(D_0\\ge2\\delta-s_0\\ge\\delta\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(0<t\\le t_1\\), \\( P(c+u,H)\\le\\frac C{t+s_N}H,\\qquad 0\\le\\mathcal S\\le C_{A,\\delta}t^{A-1}H \\) for every fixed \\(A>0\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(6.2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal S=2P^b(c,u)+\\sum_{i\\ne i'}P^b(c_i,c_{i'})\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Take \\(s_0\\) sufficiently small depending on \\(\\delta\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(0&lt;t\\le t_1\\),\n\\[\nP(c+u,H)\\le\\frac C{t+s_N}H,\\qquad\n0\\le\\mathcal S\\le C_{A,\\delta}t^{A-1}H\n\\]\nfor every fixed \\(A&gt;0\\). The first constant is independent of \\(\\delta,R_\\mu\\); no hot bound is assumed.</p><p>On any interval where \\(Z\\le1\\),\n\\[\nZ(t)\\le Z(r)+\\int_r^t\n\\left(\\frac{C_2}{s+s_N}Z(s)+C_{A,\\delta}s^{A-1}\\right)\\,ds,\n\\quad0\\le r\\le t\\le t_1,\n\\]\nwith \\(C_2\\) independent of \\(\\delta,R_\\mu,s_0,N\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>At a point let \\(a\\) be its main color, or zero, and \\(r=c-a\\).\nProposition \\(3\\) and the sheet gain estimate give \\(P(a,H)\\le C(t+s_N)^{-1}H\\). For every \\(M&gt;0\\), nonmain tails give \\(r\\le\\rho(t)H\\), \\(\\rho(t)\\le C_Mt^M\\). The bath is bounded by \\(CH\\). The quadratic gain estimate proves the first bound.</p><p>If \\(a\\ne0\\), Lemma \\(4.1\\) gives \\(u\\le Ce^{-c\\delta^2/t}H\\) at that position. The ordered cross-color sum is bounded by \\(2P(a,r)+P(r,r)\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\mathcal S\\le C\\left(\n\\frac{e^{-c\\delta^2/t}+\\rho(t)}{t+s_N}+\\rho(t)+\\rho(t)^2\n\\right)H.\n\\]\nThe exponential is bounded by any prescribed power of \\(t\\); take \\(M=A\\). If \\(a=0\\), only the last types remain.</p><p>Drop loss in the hot path formula. While \\(Z\\le1\\), \\(Z^2\\le Z\\), and \\(t+s_N\\le2\\). The gain estimates give the stated integral inequality. No differentiability of \\(Z\\) is assumed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are \\(\\bar s_0,T_0>0\\), uniform in \\(N,b\\), such that each \\(s_0\\le\\bar s_0\\), \\(0<T\\le T_0\\), admits a seed parameter \\(\\lambda\\in(0,L_0)\\) attaining the cap in Proposition \\(4\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Proposition \\(6.3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), \\(p&gt;C_2+1\\), and \\(A&gt;p+C_2+3\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(\\bar s_0,T_0&gt;0\\), uniform in \\(N,b\\), such that each \\(s_0\\le\\bar s_0\\), \\(0&lt;T\\le T_0\\), admits a seed parameter \\(\\lambda\\in(0,L_0)\\) attaining the cap in Proposition \\(4\\). The dormant solution exists on a common initial interval and satisfies\n\\[\nZ(t)\\le C_{A,\\delta}t^A.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For the dormant solution, \\(Y=C_dt^A\\) is a scalar supersolution of Lemma \\(4.2\\) if\n\\(C_d(A-C_2)\\ge C_{A,\\delta}\\), since \\((t+s_N)^{-1}\\le t^{-1}\\).\nAt each fixed \\(N\\), comparison begins at the regular section zero. Choose an interval with \\(C_dt^A&lt;1/2\\); the finite continuation criterion and cold/bath bounds prove existence and the dormant estimate there.</p><p>Reduce \\(T_0\\) so that \\(C_dT_0^{A-p-1}&lt;1\\). Then the dormant solution stays strictly below the cap, whereas at \\(\\lambda=L_0\\) the initial ratio is\n\\(L_0\\|\\chi\\|_H&gt;1\\).</p><p>For fixed \\(T,N,b\\), let \\(I\\subset[0,L_0]\\) consist of parameters whose solutions exist on \\([0,T]\\) and stay strictly below the cap. Continuous dependence and terminal continuation make \\(I\\) relatively open. It contains zero but excludes \\(L_0\\). Let \\(\\lambda_*\\) be the right endpoint of the component containing zero. Along \\(\\lambda_k\\uparrow\\lambda_*\\), cap bounds and fixed-approximation cold/bath bounds make the solutions Cauchy in free coordinates. The limit solves the equation at \\(\\lambda_*\\) and satisfies the nonstrict cap.</p><p>The continuous ratio has a maximum. If it were below one, continuous dependence would extend \\(I\\) past \\(\\lambda_*\\). Therefore its maximum is one. Neighborhoods of both endpoints are excluded from the endpoint choice, so \\(0&lt;\\lambda_*&lt;L_0\\). Reduce \\(T_0\\) so that the cap is at most one.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is \\(E_{t_b}(s_0)\\to0\\) as \\(s_0\\to0\\) such that, at every existing terminal time \\(t\\in[t_b,t_1]\\), \\( \\left\\|\\int_{t_b}^t \\mathcal S(s,x-(t-s)v,v)\\,ds\\right\\|_H \\le E_{t_b}(s_0). \\) This is uniform in seed, cap …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(6.4\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(0&lt;t_b&lt;t_1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(E_{t_b}(s_0)\\to0\\) as \\(s_0\\to0\\) such that, at every existing terminal time \\(t\\in[t_b,t_1]\\),\n\\[\n\\left\\|\\int_{t_b}^t\n\\mathcal S(s,x-(t-s)v,v)\\,ds\\right\\|_H\n\\le E_{t_b}(s_0).\n\\]\nThis is uniform in seed, cap horizon, approximation index, and cutoff.</p>", "proof": "<p>On \\([t_b,t_1]\\), nonmain terms satisfy \\(r\\le\\epsilon(s_0)H\\), with \\(\\epsilon(s_0)\\to0\\), while main sheet amplitudes are at most \\(C/t_b\\). Define\n\\[\n\\mathcal U_{\\mathrm{main}}(s,x,v)=\\sum_i\\mathbf1_{O_i(s)}(x)\\mathcal U_i(s,x,v),\n\\quad \\mathcal U_{\\mathrm{bath}}(v)=Ce^{-\\beta'|v|^2},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{\\mathrm{mb}}=2P(\\mathcal U_{\\mathrm{main}},\\mathcal U_{\\mathrm{bath}}).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n0\\le\\mathcal S\\le C_{t_b}\\epsilon(s_0)H+\\mathcal S_{\\mathrm{mb}},\n\\qquad \\mathcal S_{\\mathrm{mb}}\\le C_{t_b}H.\n\\]\nLarge-output decay gives\n\\[\n\\sup_{s,x,|v|&gt;R}\\mathcal S_{\\mathrm{mb}}/H\\le\\omega_{t_b}(R)\\to0.\n\\]\nFor \\(1&lt;q'&lt;q\\), applying the sheet estimate with\n\\(H_{q'}=e^{-|v|^2}\\langle v\\rangle^{-100}(1+|\\bar v|^{-q'})\\) gives\n\\[\n\\sup_{s,x,0&lt;|\\bar v|&lt;r}\\mathcal S_{\\mathrm{mb}}/H\n\\le C_{t_b,q'}r^{q-q'}.\n\\]</p><p>For retained \\(|v|\\le R\\), \\(|\\bar v|\\ge r\\), the spatial support lies in the projection of\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{n\\in\\Lambda}(n+B_{s_0}^2\\times[-1/4,1/4]).\n\\]\nA bounded-speed flight of duration at most \\(t_1\\) meets at most \\(C_R\\) cylinders. Each transverse crossing takes at most \\(2s_0/r\\). Hence the path integral is bounded by\n\\[\nC_{t_b}\\epsilon(s_0)+t_1\\omega_{t_b}(R)\n+C_{t_b,q'}t_1r^{q-q'}+C_{t_b,R}s_0/r.\n\\]\nChoose \\(R\\) large, then \\(r\\) small, then \\(s_0\\) small.</p><p>The argument uses a common pointwise majorant. Free coordinates and Fubini transfer it to almost every path and every fixed terminal section, giving the essential norm asserted.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For sufficiently small \\(s_0,T_0\\), all dormant solutions and cap solutions from Lemma \\(4.3\\), with \\(T\\le T_0\\), continue to \\(t_1\\) and satisfy \\( \\sup_{0\\le t\\le t_1}Z(t)\\le\\zeta. \\) Choices are uniform in …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Proposition \\(6.5\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), \\(\\zeta\\in(0,1)\\), and the exponents in Proposition \\(4\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently small \\(s_0,T_0\\), all dormant solutions and cap solutions from Lemma \\(4.3\\), with \\(T\\le T_0\\), continue to \\(t_1\\) and satisfy\n\\[\n\\sup_{0\\le t\\le t_1}Z(t)\\le\\zeta.\n\\]\nChoices are uniform in \\(N,b,R_\\mu,T\\). The dormant estimate remains valid on its common initial interval.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(0&lt;t_b&lt;t_1\\), then require \\(T_0&lt;t_b\\). At the cap horizon,\n\\(Z(T)\\le T^p(T+s_N)\\le2T^p\\), for selected and dormant solutions. While \\(Z\\le1\\), Lemma \\(4.2\\) gives\n\\[\nZ(t)\\le2T^{p-C_2}t^{C_2}+\\widetilde C_{A,\\delta}t^A,\n\\qquad T\\le t\\le t_b.\n\\]\nFrom \\(t_b\\), retain the source path integral and use Lemma \\(4.4\\):\n\\[\nZ(t)\\le Z(t_b)+E_{t_b}(s_0)+\\int_{t_b}^t(C_2/s)Z(s)\\,ds.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\sup_{t_b\\le t\\le t_1}Z(t)\n\\le(t_1/t_b)^{C_2}\n\\left(2T_0^{p-C_2}t_b^{C_2}\n+\\widetilde C_{A,\\delta}t_b^A+E_{t_b}(s_0)\\right).\n\\]\nChoose \\(t_b\\) so that the source term after multiplication is below \\(\\zeta/6\\). Since \\(p&gt;C_2\\), choose \\(T_0\\) so that the seed term is below \\(\\zeta/6\\) and \\(2T_0^p&lt;\\zeta/2\\). Finally choose \\(s_0\\) so that the path-source term is below \\(\\zeta/6\\). All estimates are strictly below \\(\\zeta/2\\), closing \\(Z\\le1\\). At a fixed approximation, cold and bath norms stay bounded through \\(t_1\\), so continuation excludes an earlier endpoint.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "All these finite evolutions continue globally.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Proposition \\(7.4\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use dormant finite evolutions and cap solutions of Proposition \\(4\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(t\\ge t_1\\), write \\(F=c+w\\), where \\(c\\) is the active cold sum and\n  \\(w(t_1)=u(t_1)+h(t_1)\\). Between retirements,\n  \\[\n  (D_t+\\nu_F^b)w=P^b(w,w)+2P^b(c,w)+\\mathcal S_c,\\qquad\n  \\mathcal S_c=\\sum_{\\substack{i\\ne i'\\\\i,i'\\ \\mathrm{active}}}P^b(c_i,c_{i'}).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix jet parameters first. All remaining construction parameters may then be chosen as specified in the proof.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>All these finite evolutions continue globally. There are \\(B&lt;\\infty\\), \\(\\kappa&gt;0\\), independent of \\(N,b_N\\ge1,T\\), such that\n\\[\n\\|w(t)\\|_H\\le B,\\qquad \\nu_F^{b_N}(t,x,v)\\ge\\kappa,\\qquad t\\ge t_1.\n\\]\nEvery active jet satisfies\n\\[\nc_i(t)\\le e^{-\\kappa(t-t_1)}\\mathcal U_i(t)\n\\]\nthrough retirement. The sum of all retirement increments in \\(H\\)-norm tends to zero as \\(s_0\\to0\\). The hot component retains Proposition \\(4\\)'s small bound on \\([0,t_1]\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>On a bootstrap interval \\(w\\le BH\\), Lemmas \\(5.2\\)–\\(5.3\\) and the cold estimates give, for \\(Z_w=\\|w\\|_H\\),\n\\[\nZ_w(t)\\le e^{-\\kappa(t-t_1)}B_0+\\frac{C_5+C_4\\theta_0}{\\kappa}+J_0\n+2C_3(1+\\theta_0)\\int_{t_1}^t\ne^{-\\kappa(t-s)}e^{-\\kappa(s-t_1)}Z_w(s)\\,ds.\n\\]\nTake \\(\\theta_0,J_0\\le1\\), discard the first exponential in the integrand, and apply Gronwall:\n\\[\nZ_w(t)\\le\n\\left(B_0+\\frac{C_4+C_5}{\\kappa}+1\\right)\ne^{4C_3/\\kappa}=:B_*.\n\\]\nThe constants defining \\(B_*\\) are fixed before \\(B\\).</p><p>Choose parameters in this order. Fix jet and kernel parameters, width factor, \\(\\mu,t_1,p\\), and \\(B_0\\). The proof of Lemma \\(5.2\\) fixes \\(\\kappa\\) before its entropy tolerance. Fix \\(C_3,C_4,C_5\\), then choose\n\\[\nB&gt;\\max(B_0+2,B_*+3).\n\\]\nPrescribe the entropy, hot, and geometric smallness required by Lemmas \\(5.2\\)–\\(5.3\\). Realize the entropy tolerance by choosing \\(\\delta\\) small and \\(R_\\mu\\) large, then \\(s_0\\) small. Apply Proposition \\(4\\) for the hot tolerance: choose \\(t_b,T_0\\) after \\(\\delta,\\zeta\\), then reduce \\(s_0\\). Further reductions meet separation, cold, and retirement requirements. None depends on approximation or horizon.</p><p>Between retirements norm continuity and \\(Z_w\\le B_*&lt;B-3\\) prevent reaching \\(B\\). At a retirement, use only the left bootstrap and obtain\n\\[\nZ_w(L_s+)\\le Z_w(L_s-)+J_s\\le B_*+1&lt;B-2,\n\\]\nrestarting the bootstrap without assuming it across the jump.</p><p>At fixed \\(N\\), only finitely many scales occur; their barriers bound the active cold norm on every finite interval. The uniform \\(w\\)-bound therefore bounds the total norm, excluding a finite maximal time. After finitely many retirements \\(F=w\\). This proves global continuation and all asserted estimates.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\int_VA(s,x-\\tau v)\\,dv\\le C_{V,\\rho}a_*,\\qquad\\tau\\ge\\rho. \\) For fixed \\(A_*\\), the following tend to zero as \\(a_*\\to0\\):", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(7.1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A:[0,\\infty)\\times(\\mathbb R^3/\\Lambda)\\to[0,\\infty)\\) be measurable,\n  \\[\n  \\sup_s\\|A(s)\\|_\\infty\\le A_*,\\qquad \\sup_s\\|A(s)\\|_1\\le a_*.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a bounded velocity set \\(V\\), \\(\\rho&gt;0\\), and, for the damped assertion, \\(\\kappa,t_1&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\int_VA(s,x-\\tau v)\\,dv\\le C_{V,\\rho}a_*,\\qquad\\tau\\ge\\rho.\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(A_*\\), the following tend to zero as \\(a_*\\to0\\):\n\\[\n\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_{t\\ge t_1,x}\n\\int_{t_1}^te^{-\\kappa(t-s)}\n\\int H(v)A(s,x-(t-s)v)\\,dv\\,ds,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_{t\\ge t_1,x}\n\\int_0^{t_1}\\int H(v)A(s,x-(t-s)v)\\,dv\\,ds.\n\\]\nThe same assertions hold when time integrals are restricted to an interval of existence.</p>", "proof": "<p>Contain \\(V\\) in a ball of radius \\(R\\). The substitution \\(z=x-\\tau v\\) gives \\(\\tau^{-3}\\), and the image meets at most \\(C(1+\\tau R)^3\\) fundamental cells. Therefore\n\\[\n\\int_VA(s,x-\\tau v)\\,dv\n\\le C\\tau^{-3}(1+\\tau R)^3\\|A(s)\\|_1.\n\\]\nThe constant is uniform for \\(\\tau\\ge\\rho\\), including arbitrarily large \\(\\tau\\).</p><p>Put \\(H_L=\\min(H,L)\\mathbf1_{\\{|v|\\le L\\}}\\) and\n\\(\\delta_L=\\int(H-H_L)\\to0\\). For \\(\\tau\\ge\\rho\\),\n\\[\n\\int H(v)A(s,x-\\tau v)\\,dv\\le A_*\\delta_L+C_{L,\\rho}a_*.\n\\]\nFor \\(\\tau&lt;\\rho\\), use \\(A_*\\int H\\). The two displayed expressions are bounded respectively by\n\\[\n\\rho A_*\\int H+\\kappa^{-1}(A_*\\delta_L+C_{L,\\rho}a_*),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\rho A_*\\int H+t_1(A_*\\delta_L+C_{L,\\rho}a_*).\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\rho\\) small, then \\(L\\) large, then \\(a_*\\) small.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "If \\(\\varepsilon_E\\) is sufficiently small depending on \\(B\\), then \\( \\nu_F^b(t,x,v)\\ge\\kappa,\\qquad t\\ge t_1, \\) where \\(\\kappa>0\\) depends only on \\(\\mu,t_1\\), not on \\(B,b,N\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(7.2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The early bounds give \\(\\|u+h\\|_H\\le B_0\\) on \\([0,t_1]\\), with \\(B_0\\) independent of the small construction parameters.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(B&gt;B_0+2\\), and consider an existing interval beginning at \\(t_1\\) on which \\(0\\le w\\le BH\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume the entropy error \\(\\sup_t\\|D_F(t)\\|_1\\le\\varepsilon_E\\) from Proposition \\(2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(\\varepsilon_E\\) is sufficiently small depending on \\(B\\), then\n\\[\n\\nu_F^b(t,x,v)\\ge\\kappa,\\qquad t\\ge t_1,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\kappa&gt;0\\) depends only on \\(\\mu,t_1\\), not on \\(B,b,N\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Under the bootstrap, \\(D_F(t,x)\\le C(B)\\) for \\(t\\ge t_1/2\\): use the early bound before \\(t_1\\), \\(w\\le BH\\) afterward, one main color of moment at most \\(2C/t_1\\), and nonmain tails.</p><p>Fix closed velocity balls \\(V_1,V_2\\) of positive radius with distance greater than three. On \\(V=V_1\\cup V_2\\),\n\\[\nP^b(\\mu,\\mu)=\\mu\\nu_\\mu^b\\ge\\mu\\nu_\\mu^1\\ge m_0&gt;0.\n\\]\nWith \\(d=(\\mu-F)_+\\), positivity gives\n\\[\nP^b(F,F)\\ge P^b(\\mu,\\mu)-2P(\\mu,d).\n\\]\nThe plane Gaussian integral is bounded, so\n\\(P(\\mu,d)(v)\\le C\\int d(p)/|p-v|\\,dp\\). Split at radius \\(r\\): near cost is \\(Cr^2\\), since \\(d\\le\\mu\\le1\\); far cost is \\(Cr^{-1}D_F\\). Fix \\(r\\) so the near cost is at most \\(m_0/2\\). Thus on \\(V\\),\n\\[\nP^b(F,F)\\ge m_*-CD_F,\\qquad \\nu_F^b\\le C+CD_F,\\quad m_*=m_0/2.\n\\]</p><p>Put \\(\\Delta=t_1/2\\), \\(X_\\tau=x-(t-\\tau)v\\). Retaining gains over\n\\(s\\in[t-\\Delta,t-\\Delta/2]\\) in the total Duhamel formula gives an integrand bounded below by\n\\[\nm_*e^{-C\\Delta}\\left(1-C\\int_s^tD_F(\\tau,X_\\tau)\\,d\\tau\\right)\n-CD_F(s,X_s).\n\\]\nIntegrate over \\(s\\) and either \\(V_k\\). The constant contributes\n\\(a_k=|V_k|(\\Delta/2)m_*e^{-C\\Delta}&gt;0\\). For \\(t-\\tau\\ge\\rho\\), Lemma \\(5.1\\) costs \\(C_\\rho\\varepsilon_E\\); the remaining short terminal strip costs \\(C(B)\\rho\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\int_{V_k}F(t,x,v)\\,dv\\ge a_k-C_\\rho\\varepsilon_E-C(B)\\rho.\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\rho\\) after \\(B\\), then \\(\\varepsilon_E\\), to obtain mass at least \\(a_k/2\\) in both balls. For any \\(v\\), one ball is at distance at least one, so\n\\[\n\\nu_F^b(v)\\ge\\pi\\min(a_1,a_2)=:\\kappa.\n\\]\nOnly the entropy tolerance depends on \\(B\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After taking \\(\\varepsilon_E,\\zeta,s_0\\) sufficiently small depending on \\(B\\), \\( P^b(w,w)\\le C_5H,\\qquad C_5=\\|P(\\mu,\\mu)\\|_H+1. \\) The constant \\(C_5\\) is independent of \\(B\\), approximation, and horizon.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(7.3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(B&gt;B_0+2\\) and suppose \\(w\\le BH\\) on an interval beginning at \\(t_1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After taking \\(\\varepsilon_E,\\zeta,s_0\\) sufficiently small depending on \\(B\\),\n\\[\nP^b(w,w)\\le C_5H,\\qquad C_5=\\|P(\\mu,\\mu)\\|_H+1.\n\\]\nThe constant \\(C_5\\) is independent of \\(B\\), approximation, and horizon. The estimate also holds at the relevant one-sided retirement sections.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose entropy small enough for Lemma \\(5.2\\). Proposition \\(3\\) then gives exponential cold decay. Main sheet amplitudes and nonmain tails imply, with constants independent of \\(B\\),\n\\[\nP(c,H)\\le C_3e^{-\\kappa(t-t_1)}H(\\mathbf1_{O(t)}+\\theta_0),\n\\qquad \\mathcal S_c\\le C_4\\theta_0H.\n\\]\nAt retirement, the increment is bounded by \\(J_sH\\), where\n\\[\nJ_s=Cs^{-C}e^{-\\kappa(L_s-t_1)},\\qquad\n\\sum_sJ_s\\le J_0(s_0)\\to0.\n\\]\nThe exponential dominates every power of \\(s\\), so, for small \\(s\\), \\(J_s\\le s^2\\).</p><p>Set\n\\[\nj=(w-\\mu)_+,\\qquad m_j=\\int j\\,dv,\\qquad\nC_\\mu=\\|\\mu\\|_H,\\quad A_B=2C_\\mu+B.\n\\]\nSince \\(w\\le F\\),\n\\[\n0\\le m_j\\le B\\int H,\\qquad \\|m_j(t)\\|_1\\le\\varepsilon_E.\n\\]\nPositivity gives\n\\[\nP^b(w,w)-P^b(\\mu,\\mu)\n\\le2P(\\mu,j)+P(j,j)\\le A_BP(H,j),\n\\]\nand the frequency deficit satisfies\n\\[\n(\\nu_\\mu^b-\\nu_F^b)\\mu\\le CHD_-.\n\\]\nSubtract the stationary Maxwellian equation and take positive parts along flights. Lemma \\(1.7\\) gives, for each \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\), a source bounded by \\(H(v)R(s,x)\\), where\n\\[\nR=\\epsilon+C_{B,\\epsilon}m_j+CD_-+C(B)(\\mathbf1_{O(s)}+\\theta_0).\n\\]\nRetirement increments of \\(j\\) are at most \\(J_sH\\).</p><p>For \\(0\\le s\\le t_1\\), set \\(j_u=(u-\\mu)_+\\), \\(m_{j_u}=\\int j_u\\). Then \\(j_u(0)=0\\),\n\\[\nj_u\\le B_0H,\\quad \\|m_{j_u}\\|_\\infty\\le B_0\\int H,\\quad\n\\|m_{j_u}(s)\\|_1\\le\\varepsilon_E.\n\\]\nSubtracting the Maxwellian equation in the bath equation, and dropping damping, bounds its source by\n\\[\nH R_u,\\qquad R_u=\\epsilon+C_{B_0,\\epsilon}m_{j_u}+CD_-.\n\\]\nThere is no positive cold gain in this bath equation.</p><p>At \\(t_1\\), \\(j(t_1)\\le j_u(t_1)+\\zeta H\\). Continue the same backward flight through \\(t_1\\); with \\(X_s=x-(t-s)v\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{j(t,x,v)}{H(v)}\n\\le\\zeta+J_0+\\int_0^{t_1}R_u(s,X_s)\\,ds\n+\\int_{t_1}^te^{-\\kappa(t-s)}R(s,X_s)\\,ds.\n\\]\nVelocity integration of the constant contributions gives\n\\[\n\\left(\\int H\\right)\n\\bigl[\\zeta+J_0+\\epsilon(t_1+\\kappa^{-1})+\\kappa^{-1}C(B)\\theta_0\\bigr].\n\\]\nAll other spatial functions have fixed pointwise bounds after \\(B\\) is fixed, and spatial integrals bounded by \\(\\varepsilon_E\\) or \\(Cs_0^{3/2}\\). Apply Lemma \\(5.1\\) to each.</p><p>For any target \\(\\tau&gt;0\\), choose \\(\\epsilon\\) first; then its coefficients are fixed. Choose the endpoint time deletions and velocity truncations in Lemma \\(5.1\\), then \\(\\varepsilon_E,s_0\\), and finally \\(\\zeta\\) and smaller \\(s_0\\). This makes\n\\[\nm_j(t,x)\\le\\tau\n\\]\nuniformly, including pre-retirement sections.</p><p>Apply Lemma \\(1.7\\) again with \\(\\epsilon'=1/(2A_B)\\), and choose\n\\[\n\\tau=\\frac1{2A_B\\max(1,C_{B,\\epsilon'})}.\n\\]\nThen \\(A_BP(H,j)\\le H\\), giving the assertion. The fixed Maxwellian gain has finite \\(H\\)-norm since \\(P(\\mu,\\mu)=\\mu\\nu_\\mu\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "A subsequence converges weakly in \\(L^1([0,\\tau]\\times\\Omega)\\) for every finite \\(\\tau\\) to a nonnegative \\(F\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Proposition \\(8.1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix parameters supplied by Proposition \\(5\\), let \\(b_N\\to\\infty\\), and take either dormant finite solutions or cap solutions at one fixed horizon \\(T\\in(0,T_0]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Denote their total densities by \\(F_N\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A subsequence converges weakly in \\(L^1([0,\\tau]\\times\\Omega)\\) for every finite \\(\\tau\\) to a nonnegative \\(F\\). The subsequence may be chosen with\n\\[\nF_N(t)\\rightharpoonup F(t)\\quad\\text{in }L^1(\\Omega)\n\\]\nat every \\(t\\ge0\\), and uniformly small polynomially weighted velocity tails on bounded time intervals.</p><p>The limit has datum \\(F_0\\), is an entropy solution with local conservation in the sense stated in Theorem \\(1\\), and\n\\[\nF\\in C([0,\\infty);L^1(\\Omega)),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\int_0^\\tau\\!\\!\\int_\\Omega\n\\langle v\\rangle^k\\bigl(F+Q^+(F,F)+Q^-(F,F)\\bigr)\\,dx\\,dv\\,dt&lt;\\infty\n\\]\nfor all \\(k\\ge0\\), \\(\\tau&lt;\\infty\\). Total mass, momentum, and energy are conserved at every time. Dormant limits retain the common early pointwise majorant.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(Q_N^\\pm=Q^{\\pm,b_N}(F_N,F_N)\\) and\n\\(R_{k,N}(t,x)=\\int\\langle v\\rangle^kF_N\\,dv\\).</p><p>For each scale \\(s\\), let \\(A_s(t)\\) be its active enlargement union in one cell, empty after retirement. On \\([0,\\tau]\\),\n\\[\n|A_s(t)|\\le C_\\tau s^2,\\qquad\nR_{k,N}\\le C_{k,\\tau}\\left(1+\\sum_{j\\ge1}\n\\frac{\\mathbf1_{A_{s_j}(t)}}{t+s_j}\\right).\n\\]\nActive sets are disjoint. Squaring creates no products between distinct singular terms, and one scale costs\n\\[\n\\int_0^\\tau\\frac{C_\\tau s^2}{(t+s)^2}\\,dt\\le C_\\tau s.\n\\]\nFor \\(A_{&lt;\\epsilon}=\\bigcup_{s_j&lt;\\epsilon}A_{s_j}\\), consequently\n\\[\n\\sup_N\\int_0^\\tau\\!\\!\\int_{A_{&lt;\\epsilon}(t)}R_{k,N}^2\n\\le C_{k,\\tau}\\sum_{s_j&lt;\\epsilon}(s_j+s_j^2)\\to0,\n\\]\nand the corresponding first-power estimate holds uniformly at each time. Outside these sets only finitely many positive thermal widths can be main, so\n\\[\nF_N(t,x,v)\\le\\Psi_{\\epsilon,\\tau}(v)\\le C_{\\epsilon,\\tau}H(v).\n\\]\nThese estimates prove uniform integrability and weighted velocity tightness of \\(F_N\\) and of\n\\[\n\\langle v\\rangle^k\\langle v_*\\rangle^kF_N(t,x,v)F_N(t,x,v_*).\n\\]\nThey also give\n\\[\n\\int\\langle v\\rangle^kQ_N^\\pm\\,dv\\le C_kR_{k+1,N}^2.\n\\]\nOn the complement of the removed sets, gains and losses are bounded by\n\\(P(\\Psi_{\\epsilon,\\tau},\\Psi_{\\epsilon,\\tau})\\) and\n\\(\\Psi_{\\epsilon,\\tau}\\nu_{\\Psi_{\\epsilon,\\tau}}\\). Thus \\(Q_N^\\pm\\) are uniformly integrable with every polynomial weight, and their integrals on short time intervals vanish uniformly.</p><p>Extract \\(F_N\\rightharpoonup F\\). Lemma \\(6.1\\), applied to\n\\(D_tF_N=Q_N^+-Q_N^-\\), gives strong local \\(L^1\\) convergence of compact velocity averages, after a countable diagonal extraction. Let\n\\(W_N=F_N(v)F_N(v_*)\\). Uniform integrability gives a weak \\(L^1\\) limit \\(W\\). Against separated tests \\(\\psi(v)\\chi(v_*)\\), the integrated product is the product of two strongly convergent averages. It converges in measure and is uniformly integrable, hence converges in \\(L^1\\) by Vitali. A countable determining family and density of finite separated sums identify\n\\[\nW=F(t,x,v)F(t,x,v_*).\n\\]\nRemove boundary time strips by uniform integrability and velocity cutoffs by weighted tightness.</p><p>Adjoin the finite angular measure and use the measure-preserving elastic substitution. On bounded velocities the cutoff is eventually absent; higher moments remove the complement. This identifies\n\\[\nQ_N^\\pm\\rightharpoonup Q^\\pm(F,F)\n\\]\nin weighted \\(L^1\\), including bounded measurable tests. Initial \\(L^1\\) convergence passes the unrenormalized equation with datum \\(F_0\\).</p><p>For a smooth compact-velocity test \\(\\varphi\\), the finite equations give\n\\[\n|\\langle F_N(t)-F_N(s),\\varphi\\rangle|\n\\le\\int_s^t\\!\\!\\int\n\\bigl(F_N|v\\cdot\\nabla_x\\varphi|+(Q_N^++Q_N^-)|\\varphi|\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe right side has a common modulus. Arzelà–Ascoli for countably many tests gives uniform convergence of pairings. At every time, the first-power small-scale estimate and fixed majorant give weak \\(L^1\\) compactness; all cluster points have these pairings and coincide. Thus one subsequence converges at every time. Weighted tightness passes conserved moments and the initial trace.</p><p>For dissipation, \\(\\Phi(A,B)=(A-B)\\log(A/B)\\) is jointly convex and lower-semicontinuous, being the sum of two convex perspectives. Joint weak convergence of primed and unprimed products on bounded collision domains, together with\n\\(\\Phi(cA,cB)=c\\Phi(A,B)\\), gives weak lower semicontinuity of integrated dissipation on \\([0,t]\\). Exhaust velocities, and combine with lower semicontinuity of \\(\\mathcal H_M\\) and initial entropy convergence:\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_M(F(t))+\\int_0^t\\mathcal D(F(s))\\,ds\\le\\mathcal H_M(F_0)\n\\]\nfor every time, without a new extraction. The negative part of \\(F\\log F\\) is bounded by mass, energy, and a Gaussian integral: above \\(e^{-1-|v|^2}\\), use\n\\(-F\\log F\\le(1+|v|^2)F\\); below it, use\n\\((1+|v|^2)e^{-1-|v|^2}\\). Relative entropy controls the positive part.</p><p>In free coordinates, the integrable unrenormalized source gives the \\(L^1\\) identity\n\\[\nF(t,x+tv,v)=F_0(x,v)+\\int_0^tQ(F,F)(s,x+sv,v)\\,ds.\n\\]\nIts right side is absolutely continuous in \\(L^1\\). Strong continuity of free transport gives a strongly continuous representative, agreeing at every time with the preceding one. The same argument works with every polynomial weight.</p><p>On almost every path, the ordinary chain rule gives\n\\[\nD_t[M\\beta(F/M)]=\\beta'(F/M)Q(F,F).\n\\]\nThe inequalities\n\\[\n|M\\beta(f/M)-M\\beta(g/M)|\\le C_\\beta|f-g|,\n\\qquad |M\\beta(f/M)|\\le C_\\beta f\n\\]\npass traces and prove the required renormalized identity. Separate renormalized collision integrability follows from \\(Q^\\pm/(1+F/M)\\le Q^\\pm\\).</p><p>Finally pass the finite local invariant balances using polynomially weighted weak convergence and initial weighted convergence. The energy flux requires \\(|v|^3\\), already controlled. Thus there is no moment defect.</p><p>Diagonalize over finite observation intervals and integer moment orders. Dormant common pointwise majorants pass by nonnegative testing, in spacetime and at each time using fixed-time weak convergence. This proves all assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The averages \\(\\int\\psi(v)f_n(t,x,v)\\,dv\\) are relatively compact in local \\(L^1_{t,x}\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(8.2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(f_n,g_n\\) be locally uniformly integrable in \\(L^1\\) on an open time-space-velocity region and\n  \\[\n  (\\partial_t+v\\cdot\\nabla_x)f_n=g_n\n  \\]\n  there in distributions.</p><p class=\"li\">The spatial domain is a flat torus or an open subset of \\(\\mathbb R^3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\psi\\) be smooth and compactly supported in velocity, with the hypotheses valid in a neighborhood of its support.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The averages \\(\\int\\psi(v)f_n(t,x,v)\\,dv\\) are relatively compact in local \\(L^1_{t,x}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Localize with smooth cutoffs in time, space, and velocity; call the localized functions \\(q_n\\), extend by zero, and set\n\\[\nr_n=(1+\\partial_t+v\\cdot\\nabla_x)q_n.\n\\]\nThe \\(r_n\\) are uniformly integrable and supported in fixed bounded time and velocity sets, and in bounded space in the Euclidean case. Integration from before the support gives\n\\[\nq_n(t,x,v)=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-s}r_n(t-s,x-sv,v)\\,ds=:\\mathcal Rr_n.\n\\]\nThe operator \\(\\mathcal R\\) is an \\(L^1\\) contraction.</p><p>Truncate \\(r_n\\) at height \\(L\\), preserving sign, to \\(r_n^{(L)}\\). Uniform integrability gives\n\\[\n\\sup_n\\|r_n-r_n^{(L)}\\|_1\\to0,\\qquad\n\\sup_n\\|r_n^{(L)}\\|_2&lt;\\infty\n\\]\nfor fixed \\(L\\). It remains to prove compactness for \\(u_n=\\mathcal Rr_n^{(L)}\\).</p><p>Fourier transformation in \\((t,x)\\), or Fourier series in periodic space, yields\n\\[\n\\left|\\int\\psi(v)\\widehat u_n(\\sigma,k,v)\\,dv\\right|^2\n\\le I_\\psi(\\sigma,k)\\int|\\widehat r_n^{(L)}(\\sigma,k,v)|^2\\,dv,\n\\]\n\\[\nI_\\psi(\\sigma,k)=\\int\\frac{|\\psi(v)|^2}{1+(\\sigma+k\\cdot v)^2}\\,dv.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\operatorname{supp}\\psi\\subset\\{|v|\\le R_0\\}\\), then\n\\(I_\\psi\\le C_\\psi/|k|\\) for \\(k\\ne0\\), while for\n\\(|\\sigma|&gt;2R_0|k|\\), \\(I_\\psi\\le C_\\psi/(1+|\\sigma|^2)\\).\nThus \\(I_\\psi\\to0\\) uniformly at high frequency. Plancherel gives uniformly small high-frequency tails.</p><p>Smooth convolution approximates the averages uniformly in \\(L^2\\). On compact time-space sets, convolutions are bounded and equicontinuous by Cauchy–Schwarz applied to translated kernels and derivatives; hence relatively compact. This proves local \\(L^2\\), then local \\(L^1\\), compactness at fixed \\(L\\). The uniform \\(L^1\\) truncation error completes the proof, also for a rotated torus.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "A subsequence has \\( h_{r_N}\\rightharpoonup g \\quad\\text{in local spacetime }L^1((0,U)\\times\\mathbb R_x^3\\times\\mathbb R_v^3), \\) where \\( 0\\le g\\le(t+l)H,\\qquad D_tg=2P(B,g)-g\\nu_B. \\) Both collision terms are locally …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Proposition \\(9.1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose cap solutions with horizons \\(T_N\\downarrow0\\), \\(s_N/T_N\\to0\\), \\(b_N\\to\\infty\\), and cap times \\(t_{*,N}\\in[0,T_N]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  m_N=T_N^p,\\quad r_N=t_{*,N}+s_N,\\quad\n  h_{r_N}(t,x,v)=\\frac{h_N(r_Nt,r_Nx,v)}{m_Nr_N},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  C_{r_N}(t,x,v)=r_Nc_N(r_Nt,r_Nx,v),\\quad\n  l_N=s_N/r_N\\to l\\in[0,1],\\quad\n  U_N=T_N/r_N\\to U\\in[1,\\infty].\n  \\]\n  Then\n  \\[\n  0\\le h_{r_N}\\le(t+l_N)H\\quad(0\\le t\\le U_N),\\qquad\n  \\|h_{r_N}(1-l_N)\\|_H=1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">After selecting logarithmic phase, the dilated scales \\(s_j/r_N\\) converge to a geometric mesh \\(\\mathcal M\\), unbounded above. If \\(l&gt;0\\), it is\n  \\(\\{l,e^\\eta l,e^{2\\eta}l,\\ldots\\}\\); if \\(l=0\\), it is two-sided with no smallest member.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(u_{\\varrho,\\sigma}\\) solve\n  \\[\n  u=\\sigma+\\alpha\\tanh(\\gamma(z-tu)/\\varrho).\n  \\]\n  With \\(\\theta=1/4\\), on \\(\\varrho&lt;|y|&lt;e^\\eta\\varrho\\) define\n  \\[\n  B(t,x,dp)=\\sum_{\\sigma=\\pm1}b_{\\varrho,\\sigma}(t,z)\n  \\delta_{u_{\\varrho,\\sigma}(t,z)e_3}(dp),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  b_{\\varrho,\\sigma}\n  =\\frac{\\theta KS(\\gamma(z-tu_{\\varrho,\\sigma})/\\varrho)}\n  {\\varrho+t\\alpha\\gamma S(\\gamma(z-tu_{\\varrho,\\sigma})/\\varrho)}.\n  \\]\n  Set \\(B=0\\) in \\(|y|&lt;l\\) if \\(l&gt;0\\); interface and axis values are arbitrary within the same bounds.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A subsequence has\n\\[\nh_{r_N}\\rightharpoonup g\n\\quad\\text{in local spacetime }L^1((0,U)\\times\\mathbb R_x^3\\times\\mathbb R_v^3),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n0\\le g\\le(t+l)H,\\qquad\nD_tg=2P(B,g)-g\\nu_B.\n\\]\nBoth collision terms are locally integrable, with position-uniform velocity majorants on compact positive-time intervals. Compact velocity averages converge strongly in local \\(L^1_{t,x}\\). The limit has locally strongly continuous \\(L^1\\) positive-time traces and, for \\(0&lt;a&lt;t&lt;U\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(t,x,v)\n={}&amp;g(a,x-(t-a)v,v)\ne^{-\\int_a^t\\nu_B(\\tau,x-(t-\\tau)v,v)\\,d\\tau}\\\\\n&amp;+2\\int_a^tP(B,g)(s,x-(t-s)v,v)\ne^{-\\int_s^t\\nu_B(\\tau,x-(t-\\tau)v,v)\\,d\\tau}\\,ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nIf \\(l&gt;0\\), select \\(\\lambda_N\\to\\lambda_0\\); these conclusions extend to zero with\n\\[\ng(0,x,v)=\\lambda_0l\\chi(x/l,v),\n\\]\nand the formula holds with \\(a=0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The implicit profile is unique because the derivative of the defining left-minus-right expression in \\(u\\) is at least one. It is smooth,\n\\[\n|u_{\\varrho,\\sigma}-\\sigma|\\le\\alpha,\\qquad\n\\partial_zu_{\\varrho,\\sigma}\n=\\frac{\\alpha\\gamma S}{\\varrho+t\\alpha\\gamma S}\\ge0.\n\\]\nOn an active annulus,\n\\[\n\\int\\langle p\\rangle^kB(t,x,dp)\\le C_k/(t+\\varrho)\\le C_k/t.\n\\]</p><p>Fix \\(a\\le t\\le A&lt;U\\), \\(|x|\\le R\\). The cap holds on all physical times up to \\(r_NA\\). Proposition \\(3\\) gives\n\\[\n\\int\\langle p\\rangle^kC_{r_N}\\,dp\n\\le C_k((t+l_N)^{-1}+1),\n\\]\nwith uniformly decreasing tails; nonmain terms are bounded by\n\\(C_Mr_N^{M+1}t^Me^{-4|p|^2}\\).</p><p>First retain scales \\(\\varrho_{N,j}=s_j/r_N\\in[\\epsilon,R_1]\\). There are uniformly finitely many. On a free-core path, self-frequency is at most \\(Cd_s/s\\); the cold remainder has moment amplitude \\(O(s^{15/2})\\); nonmain, bath, and capped hot terms give bounded additional frequencies. Physical optical depth is at most\n\\[\nC_Ar_N(d_s/s+s^{15/2}+1)=o(1).\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\ne^{-\\epsilon_N}f_i\\le c_i\\le f_i+\\epsilon_sm_i,\\qquad\\epsilon_N\\to0.\n\\]\nAfter multiplication by \\(r_N\\), the remainder has mass\n\\(O(r_N^{17/2}\\varrho_{N,j}^{15/2})\\). Hence actual colors and free cores agree in velocity total variation asymptotically on retained scales.</p><p>On bounded dilated spatial sets, only the origin copy can be main: another copy would require\n\\(|\\bar n|\\le s_0+o(1)\\), \\(|n_3|\\le3/16+o(1)\\), contradicting \\(|n|\\ge1\\).</p><p>For a centered free core, the parameter map is\n\\[\n\\bar p=d_s\\bar w,\\qquad\np_3=\\sigma+\\alpha\\tanh(\\gamma(z-tp_3)/\\varrho_N)+d_sw_3.\n\\]\nIts exact velocity integral against \\(\\psi\\) is\n\\[\n\\int J(w)\n\\frac{KS(\\gamma(z-tp_3)/\\varrho_N)}\n{\\varrho_N+t\\alpha\\gamma S(\\gamma(z-tp_3)/\\varrho_N)}\n\\psi(d_s\\bar w,p_3)\n\\mathbf1_{E_j^\\sigma}(r_Ny-r_Ntd_s\\bar w)\n\\mathbf1_{\\{|r_N(z-tp_3)|&lt;1/8\\}}\\,dw.\n\\]\nThe axial mask becomes one, and\n\\(|p_3-u_{\\varrho_N,\\sigma}|\\le d_s|w_3|\\). The smooth amplitude and velocity test converge uniformly on \\(\\operatorname{supp}J\\).</p><p>Dilated stripe period is \\(s^2/r_N=r_N\\varrho_N^2\\to0\\). Its shift-to-period ratio is at most\n\\(Ad_s/(r_N\\varrho_N^2)=Ad_0r_N^9\\varrho_N^8\\to0\\). Removing stripe endpoint neighborhoods and annular margins has vanishing cost. If \\(q_{\\mathrm{str}}\\) is a stripe indicator of mean \\(\\theta\\), and \\(Q_{\\mathrm{str}}\\) is a bounded periodic primitive of \\(q_{\\mathrm{str}}-\\theta\\), then\n\\[\n\\left|\\int f(y_1)(q_{\\mathrm{str}}(y_1/p+\\zeta)-\\theta)\\,dy_1\\right|\n\\le p\\|Q_{\\mathrm{str}}\\|_\\infty\\|f'\\|_1.\n\\]\nFubini and \\(L^1\\) approximation give weak-* stripe averaging, producing precisely \\(b_{\\varrho,\\sigma}\\).</p><p>Small scales occupy \\(|y|&lt;C\\epsilon\\), whose integrated cost on \\(t\\ge a\\) is \\(O(\\epsilon^2)\\). Large scales have no main color in the spatial compact; escaped tails vanish. Therefore\n\\[\n\\int C_{r_N}(t,x,p)\\psi(p)\\,dp\n\\rightharpoonup^*\\int\\psi\\,dB\n\\]\nin local \\(L^\\infty_{t,x}\\), including polynomial tail removal. If \\(l&gt;0\\), these bounds hold down to zero.</p><p>The rescaled hot equation is exactly\n\\[\nD_th_{r_N}=2P^{b_N}(C_{r_N},h_{r_N})\n-h_{r_N}\\nu_{C_{r_N}}^{b_N}+E_N,\n\\]\nwhere, with \\(u_N^r(t,x,v)=u_N(r_Nt,r_Nx,v)\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_N={}&amp;r_N[2P^{b_N}(u_N^r,h_{r_N})-h_{r_N}\\nu_{u_N^r}^{b_N}]\\\\\n&amp;+m_Nr_N^2[P^{b_N}(h_{r_N},h_{r_N})\n-h_{r_N}\\nu_{h_{r_N}}^{b_N}]\n+m_N^{-1}\\mathcal S_N(r_Nt,r_Nx,v).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nFor bounded dilated times and \\(M&gt;p+1\\),\n\\[\n|E_N|\\le C_A(r_N+m_Nr_N^2+T_N^{M-1-p})\\langle v\\rangle H.\n\\]\nHere \\(r_N\\le2T_N\\), and the arbitrary-power source estimate handles the last term. Thus all errors vanish locally, including down to zero. Sheet bounds and the cap give\n\\[\n|D_th_{r_N}|\\le C_{a,A}\\langle v\\rangle H.\n\\]\nWeak compactness and Lemma \\(6.1\\) yield \\(g\\) and strongly convergent hot averages.</p><p>For separated velocity tests, cold averages converge weak-* and hot averages strongly in \\(L^1\\). Their products therefore converge. Finite separated sums and uniform product mass give\n\\[\nC_{r_N}(t,x,p)h_{r_N}(t,x,v)\\,dp\\,dv\\,dx\\,dt\n\\rightharpoonup B(t,x,dp)g(t,x,v)\\,dv\\,dx\\,dt\n\\]\nagainst continuous compact velocity tests, with polynomial tails removable.</p><p>For an output test \\(\\phi\\), use the continuous incoming-pair test\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_\\phi(p,v)=\\frac{|p-v|}{2}\\int_{S^2}\n\\phi\\left(\\frac{p+v}{2}+\\frac{|p-v|}{2}\\omega\\right)\\,d\\omega,\n\\]\nbounded by \\(2\\pi|p-v|\\|\\phi\\|_\\infty\\); loss uses \\(2\\pi|p-v|\\phi(v)\\).\nThis passes both collision terms and removes the cutoff. The gain has no axis mass: each nondegenerate diameter sphere meets the axis in at most two points, and degenerate spheres have zero rate. Off the axis, the plane formula and sheet estimate give density and\n\\(P(B,g)\\le C_{a,A}H\\), \\(g\\nu_B\\le C_{a,A}\\langle v\\rangle H\\).\nThis proves the limiting equation.</p><p>If \\(l&gt;0\\), initial rescaled hot data are exactly\n\\(\\lambda_Nl_N\\chi(x/l_N,v)\\) on each fixed compact, converging strongly to the asserted trace. Uniform source bounds pass the equation at zero.</p><p>Finally change to free coordinates on bounded velocities and enlarged spatial compacts. The integrable source gives local strong continuity; velocity tails remove truncations. The cap passes to every trace. Since\n\\(\\nu_B\\le C_a\\langle v\\rangle\\) for \\(t\\ge a\\), the scalar integrating-factor formula gives the displayed mild identity. Fubini shows that arbitrary interface values do not change it.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( g\\not\\equiv0 \\quad\\text{on }(0,U)\\times\\mathbb R_x^3\\times\\mathbb R_v^3. \\) This includes \\(l=1\\), \\(U=1\\), and \\(U=\\infty\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Proposition \\(10.1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Take any sequence of cap solutions with \\(T_n\\to0\\), \\(N_n\\to\\infty\\), \\(s_{N_n}/T_n\\to0\\), and perform Proposition \\(7\\)'s dilation.</p><p class=\"li\">For this proposition abbreviate\n  \\[\n  h_n=h_{r_n},\\quad C_n=C_{r_n},\\quad\n  \\ell_n=s_{N_n}/r_n,\\quad\\tau_n=1-\\ell_n,\\quad\n  U_n=T_n/r_n,\\quad f_n=h_n/H.\n  \\]\n  Thus \\(0\\le f_n\\le t+\\ell_n\\) on \\(0\\le t\\le U_n\\) and\n  \\(\\|f_n(\\tau_n)\\|_\\infty=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Pass to any subsequence supplied by Proposition \\(7\\), with limits \\(l,U,g\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\ng\\not\\equiv0\n\\quad\\text{on }(0,U)\\times\\mathbb R_x^3\\times\\mathbb R_v^3.\n\\]\nThis includes \\(l=1\\), \\(U=1\\), and \\(U=\\infty\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Select \\(\\lambda_n\\to\\lambda_\\infty\\). If \\(l&gt;0\\) and \\(\\lambda_\\infty&gt;0\\), Proposition \\(7\\) gives a nonzero initial trace. Since\n\\(\\nu_B\\le C(1+|v|)/(t+l)\\), a bounded positive portion survives with strictly positive factor and gives interior mass.</p><p>If \\(l&gt;0\\) and \\(\\lambda_\\infty=0\\), use Lemma \\(8.2\\) from zero. The initial normalized norm is at most\n\\(\\lambda_n\\ell_n\\|\\chi/H\\|_\\infty\\), and \\(t+\\ell_n\\ge l/2\\). Scalar comparison gives\n\\[\n\\sup_{0\\le t\\le\\tau_n}\\|f_n(t)\\|_\\infty\n\\le(\\lambda_n\\ell_n\\|\\chi/H\\|_\\infty+\\epsilon_n)e^{2C/l}\\to0,\n\\]\ncontradicting cap attainment. These cases include \\(l=1\\).</p><p>It remains that \\(l=0\\), so \\(\\tau_n\\to1\\). Suppose \\(g=0\\). Nonnegativity upgrades weak convergence of \\(h_n\\) to local \\(L^1\\) convergence by testing with a nonnegative function at least one on a compact. Consequently \\(f_n\\to0\\) in measure on compacts off the axis.</p><p>Fix \\(0&lt;a&lt;1/4\\). Positive comparison reads\n\\(f_n\\le d_n+\\mathcal V_nf_n+e_n\\), with\n\\[\nd_n(t,x,v)=f_n(a,x-(t-a)v,v),\\quad\n\\|d_n\\|_\\infty\\le a+\\ell_n,\\quad0\\le e_n\\le\\epsilon_n.\n\\]\nIterate five times:\n\\[\nf_n(t,x,v)\\le\n(a+\\ell_n+\\epsilon_n)\\sum_{j=0}^4\\frac{(C\\log(1/a))^j}{j!}\n+(\\mathcal V_n^5f_n)(t,x,v).\n\\]\nChoose good cap endpoints with \\(f_n(\\tau_n,x_n,v_n)\\ge1/2\\). The five-fold residual tends to zero along every subsequence: Lemma \\(8.3\\) removes escaping endpoints; convergent axial limits are handled by Lemma \\(8.6\\), with three further bounded gains; nonaxial limits are handled by Lemma \\(8.10\\).</p><p>Taking limsup yields\n\\[\n\\frac12\\le a\\sum_{j=0}^4\\frac{(C\\log(1/a))^j}{j!}.\n\\]\nLet \\(a\\downarrow0\\), a contradiction. The argument allows \\(U=\\infty\\), and its time deletion handles \\(U=1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At each position at most one summand is nonzero, and \\( \\int\\langle p\\rangle^kA_n(t,x,dp)\\le C_k/(t+\\ell_n), \\qquad P(A_n,H)\\le C(t+\\ell_n)^{-1}H. \\) There are \\(\\epsilon_n\\downarrow0\\) such that, while …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(9.2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the sequence in Proposition \\(8\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define from freely transported initial colors, on the whole interval \\(0\\le t\\le1\\),\n  \\[\n  A_n(t,x,dp)=r_n\\sum_{i:j\\le N_n}f_i(r_nt,r_nx,p)\\,dp.\n  \\]\n  This is meaningful even when \\(t&gt;U_n\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At each position at most one summand is nonzero, and\n\\[\n\\int\\langle p\\rangle^kA_n(t,x,dp)\\le C_k/(t+\\ell_n),\n\\qquad P(A_n,H)\\le C(t+\\ell_n)^{-1}H.\n\\]\nThere are \\(\\epsilon_n\\downarrow0\\) such that, while \\(0\\le t\\le\\min(1,U_n)\\),\n\\[\nD_th_n\\le2P(A_n,h_n)+\\epsilon_nH\n\\]\nalong paths, on full-measure endpoint sets at each time section.</p>", "proof": "<p>Physical free-core supports stay in disjoint enlargements through time \\(r_n\\), with no retirement for large \\(n\\). The exact Jacobian formula gives moment and sheet amplitudes\n\\(Cr_n/(r_nt+s_j)\\le C/(t+\\ell_n)\\).</p><p>In the upper cold bound, a main remainder contributes at most\n\\(Cr_ns_j^{15/2}H\\) to gain against \\(H\\); nonmain terms have a vanishing Gaussian majorant after multiplication by \\(r_n\\). More precisely,\n\\(C_n\\le A_n+R_n\\) with \\(R_n\\ge0\\) and \\(P(R_n,H)\\le o(1)H\\).\nOn the interval, \\(h_n\\le2H\\). Drop losses in the rescaled hot equation, use \\(P^{b_n}\\le P\\), and bound positive bath, self-gain, and source terms as in Proposition \\(7\\). This gives the comparison. Free-coordinate Fubini supplies the asserted representatives and endpoint sets.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The measures are defined on \\(0\\le t\\le1\\), independently of the cap interval.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(10.2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the free-core measures \\(A_n\\) in Lemma \\(8.1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a fixed \\(a&gt;0\\), define\n  \\[\n  K_n(t,x,v;dp\\,d\\xi)\n  =4A_n(t,x,dp)\\delta((v-p)\\cdot(\\xi-v))\n  \\frac{H(\\xi)}{H(v)}\\,d\\xi,\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  (\\mathcal V_nF)(t,x,v)\n  =\\int_a^t\\int\n  F(s,x-(t-s)v,\\xi)\n  K_n(s,x-(t-s)v,v;dp\\,d\\xi)\\,ds.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The measures are defined on \\(0\\le t\\le1\\), independently of the cap interval. A nonzero core has dilated scale \\(\\varrho=s/r_n\\), relative transverse location\n\\(\\varrho&lt;|y|&lt;e^\\eta\\varrho\\), and parameter formula\n\\[\n\\frac{KS(\\gamma(z-tp_3)/\\varrho)}\n{\\varrho+t\\alpha\\gamma S(\\gamma(z-tp_3)/\\varrho)}J(w)\\,dw,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\bar p=d_s\\bar w,\\qquad\np_3=\\sigma+\\alpha\\tanh(\\gamma(z-tp_3)/\\varrho)+d_sw_3,\n\\]\nwith measurable masks in \\([0,1]\\). Its coefficient is at most \\(C/(t+\\varrho)\\).</p><p>The path comparison in Lemma \\(8.1\\) holds. For \\(t\\ge a\\), \\(K_n\\) has total mass at most \\(C/a\\); normalized increment, input, and Gaussian parameter tails are uniformly negligible, and its mass tends uniformly to zero for large output speed. For bounded nonnegative \\(F\\),\n\\[\n\\|\\mathcal V_n^jF(t)\\|_\\infty\n\\le\\|F\\|_\\infty\\frac{(C\\log(t/a))^j}{j!},\n\\qquad a\\le t\\le1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The geometric and parameter assertions follow from Lemma \\(8.1\\) and the exact free-core formula. Since\n\\[\n\\frac{KS}{\\varrho+t\\alpha\\gamma S}\\le C/(t+\\varrho),\n\\]\nand \\(|p_3|\\le1+\\alpha+d_s|w_3|\\), a fixed product Gaussian majorant of \\(J\\), together with Lemma \\(1.5\\), supplies uniform sheet domination. Lemmas \\(1.3\\)–\\(1.5\\) give all kernel assertions.</p><p>At each finite \\(n\\), \\(A_n\\) has a velocity density \\(a_n\\). Its hot-input marginal, for \\(\\xi\\ne v\\), is\n\\[\n\\frac{4H(\\xi)}{|\\xi-v|H(v)}\n\\left[\\int_{(v-p)\\cdot(\\xi-v)=0}a_n(t,x,p)\\,dA_p\\right]d\\xi.\n\\]\nIt has no atom at \\(\\xi=v\\). Tonelli and the measure-preserving endpoint translation show that changing inputs on a null spacetime-velocity set changes the Volterra output only on a null endpoint set, at each fixed time. Finite repetitions preserve this. Intersect good sets for countably many lower times and approximation indices.</p><p>Finally, use the kernel bound \\(C/t\\). The ordered \\(j\\)-time integral of\n\\(\\prod_{i=1}^jC/t_i\\) is \\((C\\log(t/a))^j/j!\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At arbitrarily small uniform error, all \\(|p_i|+|v_i-v_{i-1}|\\) and sheet parameters may be restricted to fixed bounded sets.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(10.3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(a&gt;0\\), \\(t_n\\in[a,1]\\), and inputs \\(0\\le F_n\\le2\\) in a five-fold integral\n  \\((\\mathcal V_n^5F_n)(t_n,x_n,v_n)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At arbitrarily small uniform error, all\n\\(|p_i|+|v_i-v_{i-1}|\\) and sheet parameters may be restricted to fixed bounded sets.\nIf \\(|v_n|\\to\\infty\\), the whole integral tends to zero. With bounded endpoint velocities, it also tends to zero if\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(x_n,r_n^{-1}\\Lambda)\\to\\infty.\n\\]\nThus a sequence of positive lower bounds has, after periodic translations and subselection, convergent endpoint positions and velocities. On retained bounded paths, only one periodic copy can occur, and physical widths are \\(O(r_n^{10})\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Delete a normalized tail at one gain, bound the remaining four gains by Lemma \\(8.2\\), and sum over five deletion locations. Large-output decay at the first gain proves the velocity assertion.</p><p>With bounded endpoint velocities and increments, all velocities are bounded by \\(V\\), and collision positions lie within \\(V\\) of the endpoint. Let\n\\(d_n=\\operatorname{dist}(x_n,r_n^{-1}\\Lambda)\\to\\infty\\).\nA first core of scale \\(\\varrho\\ge M\\) has kernel mass at most \\(C/M\\).\nFor \\(\\varrho\\le M\\), relative transverse position is bounded by \\(e^\\eta M\\), whereas distance to its period point is at least \\(d_n-V\\). Thus its relative axial coordinate tends uniformly to infinity. For bounded parameters,\n\\[\n\\frac{KS(\\gamma(z-tp_3)/\\varrho)}\n{\\varrho+t\\alpha\\gamma S(\\gamma(z-tp_3)/\\varrho)}\n\\le \\frac K\\varrho S(\\gamma(z-tp_3)/\\varrho)\\to0\n\\]\nuniformly for \\(0&lt;\\varrho\\le M\\): when \\(|z-tp_3|\\ge Z\\), bound by\n\\(4K\\varrho^{-1}e^{-2\\gamma Z/\\varrho}\\). The sheet estimate makes the first kernel small. Let \\(n\\to\\infty\\), then \\(M\\to\\infty\\), then remove tail error.</p><p>Translate to a nearby dilated lattice point. Physical enlargements for these small times lie in balls of radius less than \\(1/2\\) about their centers; distinct lattice centers are at least one apart. Bounded dilated paths have physical distance \\(O(r_n)\\), so only the chosen copy occurs. Its scales satisfy \\(s/r_n\\le C\\), hence \\(d_s=O(r_n^{10})\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every fixed \\(M,\\delta>0\\), \\( \\lim_{\\epsilon\\downarrow0}\\sup_c \\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_{|v|\\le M,\\ 0<|\\bar v|\\le\\epsilon} \\mathcal K_c(v;|\\bar\\xi|\\ge\\delta)=0, \\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(10.4\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(L&lt;\\infty\\) and \\(c\\in\\mathcal S_L\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal S_L(\\epsilon)\\) be the subclass with elementary transverse widths at most \\(\\epsilon\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed \\(M,\\delta&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\lim_{\\epsilon\\downarrow0}\\sup_c\n\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_{|v|\\le M,\\ 0&lt;|\\bar v|\\le\\epsilon}\n\\mathcal K_c(v;|\\bar\\xi|\\ge\\delta)=0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\lim_{\\epsilon\\downarrow0}\\sup_c\n\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_{|v|\\le M,\\ 0&lt;|\\bar v|\\le\\epsilon}\n\\mathcal K_c(v;|v_3-p_3|\\ge\\delta)=0.\n\\]\nFor \\(R\\ge1\\) and an axial interval \\(I\\) of length \\(0&lt;\\ell\\le1\\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_c(v;|p|+|\\xi-v|\\le R,\\ \\xi_3\\in I)\n\\le C_{q,m,L,R}(\\ell+\\ell^{2-q}).\n\\]\nConsequently the unrestricted axial input marginals are uniformly non-atomic. Finally, for \\(M&lt;\\infty\\), \\(r_0&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\lim_{\\delta\\downarrow0}\\limsup_{\\epsilon\\downarrow0}\n\\sup_{c\\in\\mathcal S_L(\\epsilon)}\n\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_{|v|\\le M,\\ |\\bar v|\\ge r_0}\n\\mathcal K_c(v;|\\bar\\xi|&lt;\\delta)=0.\n\\]\nAll statements hold under uniformly bounded amplitudes.</p>", "proof": "<p>Remove \\(|p|+|\\xi-v|&gt;R\\) by Lemma \\(1.4\\). Remaining Gaussian and polynomial weight factors are bounded.</p><p>On \\(|\\bar\\xi|\\ge\\delta\\), \\(W(\\xi)\\le1+\\delta^{-q}\\); regular line integration gives\n\\[\nC_{R,\\delta}\\frac{|\\bar v|^{-1}}{1+|\\bar v|^{-q}}\n\\le C_{R,\\delta}|\\bar v|^{q-1}.\n\\]\nFor axial mismatch at least \\(\\delta\\), integrate in \\(\\xi_3\\) and obtain\n\\(C_{R,\\delta}/W(v)\\le C_{R,\\delta}|\\bar v|^q\\).\nThe axial interval bound is Lemma \\(1.2\\); tail removal gives uniform non-atomicity.</p><p>For the final assertion, delete \\(|\\bar p|&gt;r_0/4\\). For an elementary Gaussian,\n\\[\n\\mathbf1_{\\{|\\bar p|&gt;r_0/4\\}}\\mathcal G_d\n\\le2e^{-r_0^2/(32d^2)}\\mathcal G_{\\sqrt2d},\n\\]\nso its cost vanishes with widths. On the remainder, with the notation of Lemma \\(1.2\\),\n\\[\n|n|\\ge3r_0/4,\\qquad A\\ge3r_0/5.\n\\]\nIf \\(|v_3-p_3|\\le r_0^2/(8R)\\), then\n\\(|B(\\xi_3-v_3)|\\le r_0/6\\), so the transverse line cannot meet\n\\(|\\bar\\xi|&lt;\\delta&lt;r_0/3\\). Otherwise integrate in \\(\\xi_3\\), with divisor bounded by \\(8R/r_0^2\\), to obtain\n\\(C_{R,r_0}(\\delta^2+\\delta^{2-q})\\). Let widths vanish, then \\(\\delta\\downarrow0\\), then remove tails.</p><p>The paper's Remark \\(10.5\\) explains why one gain is insufficient. For\n\\(c=\\delta_{(0,0,u)}\\), \\(v_\\epsilon=(\\epsilon,0,u)\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{P(c,H)(v_\\epsilon)}{H(v_\\epsilon)}\n=\\frac2{\\epsilon H(v_\\epsilon)}\n\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}H(\\epsilon,y,z)\\,dy\\,dz.\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(y=\\epsilon s\\) in the singular part and using dominated convergence gives the positive limit\n\\[\n2a_qe^{u^2}\\langle u\\rangle^m\n\\int_{\\mathbb R}e^{-z^2}\\langle z\\rangle^{-m}\\,dz.\n\\]\nIts axial input marginal is absolutely continuous. Thus transverse concentration is compatible with axial interval control, motivating two successive gains.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "If \\(a\\le t_2\\le t_1\\), \\(\\Delta=t_1-t_2\\), and \\(z_2=z_1-\\Delta w\\), then \\( |w-u_{\\varrho,\\sigma}(t_1,z_1)| \\le|w-u_{\\varrho,\\sigma}(t_2,z_2)|. \\) As \\(\\varrho\\downarrow0\\), the distance of \\(u_{\\varrho,\\sigma}(t,z)\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(10.6\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(u_{\\varrho,\\sigma}\\) be the implicit axial profile from Proposition \\(7\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Work on compact sets with \\(t\\ge a&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(a\\le t_2\\le t_1\\), \\(\\Delta=t_1-t_2\\), and \\(z_2=z_1-\\Delta w\\), then\n\\[\n|w-u_{\\varrho,\\sigma}(t_1,z_1)|\n\\le|w-u_{\\varrho,\\sigma}(t_2,z_2)|.\n\\]\nAs \\(\\varrho\\downarrow0\\), the distance of \\(u_{\\varrho,\\sigma}(t,z)\\) to\n\\[\n\\{z/t,\\sigma-\\alpha,\\sigma+\\alpha\\}\n\\]\ntends uniformly to zero. The perturbed parameter velocity obeys\n\\[\n|p_3-u_{\\varrho,\\sigma}(t,z)|\\le d_s|w_3|.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The profile satisfies \\(\\partial_zu\\ge0\\), \\(\\partial_tu+u\\partial_zu=0\\).\nFor fixed \\(t_1,t_2,z_1\\),\n\\[\nF(w)=w-u_{\\varrho,\\sigma}(t_2,z_1-\\Delta w)\n\\]\nhas derivative at least one. Its root is \\(u_{\\varrho,\\sigma}(t_1,z_1)\\), which stays unchanged along its defining characteristic. Integrate the derivative bound.</p><p>Fix \\(0&lt;\\delta&lt;\\alpha\\). Unless \\(u\\) is within \\(\\delta\\) of an endpoint speed,\n\\[\n|z-tu|\\le(\\varrho/\\gamma)\\operatorname{arctanh}(1-\\delta/\\alpha),\n\\]\nso\n\\[\n|u-z/t|\\le\\frac{\\varrho}{\\gamma a}\n\\operatorname{arctanh}(1-\\delta/\\alpha).\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\delta\\), then \\(\\varrho\\). The perturbed estimate follows because\n\\(p\\mapsto p-\\sigma-\\alpha\\tanh(\\gamma(z-tp)/\\varrho)\\) has derivative at least one and values \\(0,d_sw_3\\) at the two velocities.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.6", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( (\\mathcal V_n^2\\mathbf1)(t_n,x_n,v_n)\\to0. \\) The conclusion persists if later factors in a longer finite gain integral are uniformly bounded.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(10.7\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(a&gt;0\\), let \\(t_n\\in[a,1]\\), and assume bounded endpoint positions and velocities with \\(\\bar v_n\\to0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n(\\mathcal V_n^2\\mathbf1)(t_n,x_n,v_n)\\to0.\n\\]\nThe conclusion persists if later factors in a longer finite gain integral are uniformly bounded.</p>", "proof": "<p>Delete parameter and increment tails at arbitrary small cost. Retained velocities and positions lie in compact sets, times in \\([a,1]\\), and scales in \\((0,M]\\); widths tend uniformly to zero.</p><p>Let \\((t_1,y_1,z_1)\\) be the first collision point, \\(\\xi\\) its input, and\n\\(z_2=z_1-(t_1-t_2)\\xi_3\\). Lemma \\(8.4\\) gives a common modulus\n\\(\\omega(\\rho)\\downarrow0\\) for first-kernel mass in any axial interval of length \\(\\rho\\), and first-kernel mass on \\(|\\bar\\xi|&gt;b\\) tends to zero for fixed \\(b&gt;0\\).</p><p>Given error \\(\\epsilon\\), choose \\(\\delta\\) so neighborhoods of\n\\[\nz_1/t_1,\\quad1-\\alpha,\\quad1+\\alpha,\\quad-1-\\alpha,\\quad-1+\\alpha\n\\]\nhave total first-kernel mass at most \\(\\epsilon\\). Lemma \\(8.5\\) gives a uniform \\(\\varrho_0&gt;0\\) so all smaller-scale profiles are within \\(\\delta/4\\) of their collapsed speeds.</p><p>There are uniformly finitely many mesh scales in \\([\\varrho_0,M]\\). Choose \\(b_0&gt;0\\) so the union of \\(b_0\\)-neighborhoods of their two profile speeds at \\((t_1,z_1)\\) has mass at most \\(\\epsilon\\). Outside these exceptional intervals, every retained second cold speed has axial mismatch at least\n\\[\nc_*=\\tfrac14\\min(\\delta,b_0)&gt;0.\n\\]\nFor finite scales use characteristic matching and the thermal error. For constant collapsed speeds this is direct; for the \\(z/t\\) branch use\n\\[\n\\xi_3-z_2/t_2=(t_1/t_2)(\\xi_3-z_1/t_1).\n\\]</p><p>The second-kernel axial mismatch estimate tends uniformly to zero as\n\\(|\\bar\\xi|\\to0\\). Choose \\(b\\) so its mass is at most \\(\\epsilon\\) when\n\\(|\\bar\\xi|\\le b\\) and mismatch holds. The first-kernel mass on\n\\(|\\bar\\xi|&gt;b\\) tends to zero, exceptional interval mass is at most \\(2\\epsilon\\), and all remaining masses are bounded. Remove initial tails and let \\(\\epsilon\\downarrow0\\). Later bounded factors only multiply these estimates.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.7", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every nonnegative measurable \\(F\\), \\( \\int_{n^\\perp}F(k)\\frac{dA(k)}{|n|} =\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}F(n\\times L)|n|\\zeta(L\\cdot e)\\,dL. \\) If \\(\\delta\\le|n|\\le R\\) and \\(|k|\\le R_1\\), the parameter measure is supported in …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(10.8\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n\\ne0\\), \\(e=n/|n|\\), and let \\(\\zeta\\ge0\\) be bounded, supported in \\([-1,1]\\), with integral one.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every nonnegative measurable \\(F\\),\n\\[\n\\int_{n^\\perp}F(k)\\frac{dA(k)}{|n|}\n=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}F(n\\times L)|n|\\zeta(L\\cdot e)\\,dL.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\delta\\le|n|\\le R\\) and \\(|k|\\le R_1\\), the parameter measure is supported in\n\\[\n|L|\\le\\sqrt{(R_1/\\delta)^2+1}\n\\]\nand has density at most \\(R\\|\\zeta\\|_\\infty\\), before multiplication by \\(F\\). The formula may be iterated with normals measurable in earlier parameters.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(L=L^\\perp+\\lambda e\\). The map \\(L^\\perp\\mapsto n\\times L^\\perp\\) multiplies area by \\(|n|^2\\), and integration in \\(\\lambda\\) gives \\(\\int\\zeta=1\\). This proves the identity and bounds. For successive gains, condition on preceding parameters, apply the fixed-normal identity, and use Tonelli. No derivatives of variable normals enter.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.8", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\int_{\\mathcal O}|F_n(\\Phi_n(q))a_n(q)|\\,dq\\to0. \\) No convergence or continuity of \\(a_n\\) is required.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(10.9\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal O\\subset\\mathbb R^k\\) be open, \\(k\\ge d\\), and\n  \\(\\Phi_n,\\Phi:\\mathcal O\\to\\mathbb R^d\\) satisfy\n  \\(\\Phi_n\\to\\Phi\\) in \\(C^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}\\) and\n  \\(\\operatorname{rank}D\\Phi=d\\) almost everywhere.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K\\Subset\\mathcal O\\), \\(B\\subset\\mathbb R^d\\) be compact.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(a_n\\) be measurable, supported in \\(K\\), \\(|a_n|\\le A\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F_n\\) be measurable, supported in \\(B\\), \\(|F_n|\\le M\\), and \\(F_n\\to0\\) in measure.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\int_{\\mathcal O}|F_n(\\Phi_n(q))a_n(q)|\\,dq\\to0.\n\\]\nNo convergence or continuity of \\(a_n\\) is required.</p>", "proof": "<p>Boundedness on \\(B\\) and convergence in measure give \\(\\|F_n\\|_1\\to0\\).\nEnclose the null critical set in \\(K\\) by an open \\(E\\) with \\(|E\\cap K|&lt;\\epsilon\\).\nAt a regular point \\(q_0\\), choose \\(d\\) input coordinates with a nonzero minor and let \\(\\pi\\) project to the complementary coordinates. Define\n\\(G=(\\Phi,\\pi q)\\), \\(G_n=(\\Phi_n,\\pi q)\\), \\(A_0=DG(q_0)\\).\nOn a small convex ball \\(V\\),\n\\[\n\\sup_V\\|A_0^{-1}DG_n-I\\|&lt;1/2\n\\]\nfor large \\(n\\). Integration on segments makes \\(G_n\\) injective and gives a uniform positive Jacobian lower bound, hence a diffeomorphism onto its image.</p><p>Cover compact \\(K\\setminus E\\) by finitely many such balls. Change variables:\n\\[\n\\int_{V\\cap K}|F_n(\\Phi_n(q))|\\,dq\n\\le c_V^{-1}|\\pi(V)|\\|F_n\\|_1.\n\\]\nFor \\(k=d\\), the zero-dimensional factor is one. The exceptional contribution, including density, is at most \\(AM\\epsilon\\). Sum charts, pass to the limit, then let \\(\\epsilon\\downarrow0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.9", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The map \\( \\Phi:(t_1,\\ldots,t_5,L_1,\\ldots,L_5)\\mapsto(t_5,x_5,v_5) \\) is real analytic on the connected domain \\((0,\\infty)^5\\times\\mathbb R^{15}\\) and has rank seven almost everywhere, in particular on the ordered …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(10.10\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix endpoint \\((t_0,x_0,v_0)\\), \\(t_0&gt;0\\), \\(\\bar v_0\\ne0\\), five positive scales \\(\\varrho_i\\), and signs \\(\\sigma_i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(t_i&gt;0\\), \\(L_i\\in\\mathbb R^3\\), recursively define\n  \\[\n  x_i=x_{i-1}-(t_{i-1}-t_i)v_{i-1},\\quad\n  p_i=u_{\\varrho_i,\\sigma_i}(t_i,z_i)e_3,\\quad n_i=v_{i-1}-p_i,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  k_i=n_i\\times L_i,\\qquad v_i=v_{i-1}+k_i,\\quad i=1,\\ldots,5.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The map\n\\[\n\\Phi:(t_1,\\ldots,t_5,L_1,\\ldots,L_5)\\mapsto(t_5,x_5,v_5)\n\\]\nis real analytic on the connected domain \\((0,\\infty)^5\\times\\mathbb R^{15}\\) and has rank seven almost everywhere, in particular on the ordered region.</p><p>For convergent nonaxial endpoints and positive scales, the corresponding finite-temperature maps, with bounded cold parameters added, converge in \\(C^1\\) on compact parameter sets. Uniformly bounded measurable parameter densities on a common compact therefore satisfy Lemma \\(8.8\\), including measurable restrictions.</p>", "proof": "<p>The implicit analytic function theorem applies to the axial equation because its \\(u\\)-derivative is\n\\(1+(t\\alpha\\gamma/\\varrho)S&gt;0\\). Uniqueness identifies local analytic profiles. Recursion remains meaningful without ordering positive times, giving the connected domain.</p><p>Exhibit rank seven at equal times \\(t_i=\\tau\\in(0,t_0)\\). Rotate about the axis so \\(v_0=(R,0,z)\\), \\(R&gt;0\\). All collision positions coincide and \\(p_i=a_ie_3\\), with arbitrary \\(a_i\\). Choose\n\\[\nk_1=(0,1,0),\\qquad k_2=(1,-R,0).\n\\]\nThey satisfy the orthogonality constraints and span the transverse plane. With\n\\(w=\\bar v_2=(R+1,1-R)\\ne0\\), \\(b_3=v_{2,3}-a_3\\), choose small nonzero \\(\\epsilon\\) and\n\\[\nk_3=\\epsilon(-b_3w/|w|^2,1).\n\\]\nThen \\(k_3\\perp n_3\\), its axial component is nonzero, and \\(\\bar v_3\\ne0\\).\nWriting \\(\\bar v_3=(X,Y)\\), take \\(k_4=(-Y,X,0)\\), \\(k_5=0\\).\nThen \\(n_4,n_5\\) are nonparallel because\n\\(\\det(\\bar n_4,\\bar n_5)=X^2+Y^2&gt;0\\).\nRepresent increments by \\(L_i=-n_i\\times k_i/|n_i|^2\\), and choose \\(L_5=0\\).</p><p>At equal times,\n\\[\nx_5=x_0-v_0(t_0-t_1)-\\sum_{i=1}^4v_i(t_i-t_{i+1}).\n\\]\nAngular derivatives of \\(x_5\\) vanish; holding \\(t_5\\) fixed, its first three time columns are \\(-k_1,-k_2,-k_3\\), independent. Final velocity angular derivatives in \\(L_4,L_5\\) span\n\\(n_4^\\perp+n_5^\\perp=\\mathbb R^3\\). Thus a block-triangular \\(6\\times6\\) minor for \\((x_5,v_5)\\) is invertible. Append target \\(t_5\\) and its column.</p><p>This minor is a nontrivial analytic function. Its zero set is null: at every zero, a finite derivative is nonzero unless analytic continuation makes the function identically zero; derivatives of minimal order place zeros on countably many regular hypersurface charts. Rank failure is contained in that zero set. The witness persists at nearby strictly ordered times and need not obey masks.</p><p>For prelimit maps replace partners by the finite-temperature parameter formula and add \\(w_i\\). Positive scale lower bounds and implicit derivative lower bound one give \\(C^1\\) convergence on compact sets by differentiation. The limit is independent of the spectator \\(w_i\\), and has rank seven almost everywhere by Fubini. Apply Lemma \\(8.8\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.10", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( (\\mathcal V_n^5f_n)(\\tau_n,x_n,v_n)\\to0. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(10.11\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(\\ell_n\\to0\\), hit endpoints converge after periodic translation, and their limiting velocity is nonaxial.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(0\\le f_n\\le2\\) on \\(a\\le t\\le\\tau_n\\) and \\(f_n\\to0\\) in measure on every compact subset of\n  \\[\n  (0,U)\\times\\mathbb R_x^3\\times\\{\\bar v\\ne0\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n(\\mathcal V_n^5f_n)(\\tau_n,x_n,v_n)\\to0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Delete kernel tails. Paths are bounded and their transverse cold widths tend uniformly to zero. Starting from the nonaxial endpoint, successively use Lemma \\(8.4\\)'s small-input estimate at the five gains to delete\n\\(|\\bar v_i|&lt;b_i\\), with arbitrarily small total error. All retained transverse speeds have a positive lower bound \\(b\\); normals have length at least \\(b/2\\).</p><p>Delete collision positions \\(|y_i|&lt;\\rho\\). Conditional on previous parameters, \\(y_i\\) is affine in \\(t_i\\), with derivative \\(\\bar v_{i-1}\\), so its time set has length at most \\(2\\rho/b\\). Kernel mass bounds give deletion cost \\(C_a\\rho/b\\). Retained scales now lie in a compact positive interval. Mesh convergence leaves finitely many five-tuples of scales and signs, with positive limits.</p><p>If \\(U=1\\), delete \\(t_5&gt;1-\\delta\\). Ordering puts all times in an interval of length at most \\(\\delta\\), costing at most\n\\(2(C/a)^5\\delta^5/5!\\). If \\(U&gt;1\\), no such deletion is needed. Delete thin strips near \\(t_5=a\\) and consecutive time coincidences, at cost \\(C_a\\kappa\\). The remaining times lie in a fixed compact subset of the strict ordered region.</p><p>For each retained scale/sign tuple, construct maps without spatial masks but keep masks in the integrand. Lemma \\(8.7\\) converts each plane integral into three redundant \\(L_i\\)-coordinates. Five times, fifteen \\(L\\)-coordinates, and fifteen cold parameters lie in a common compact. Parameter density is uniformly bounded: amplitudes are at most \\(C/a\\), normals are bounded above and below, and retained ratios \\(H(v_i)/H(v_{i-1})\\) are bounded. All masks and restrictions are measurable factors in \\([0,1]\\).</p><p>Actual targets \\((t_5,x_5,v_5)\\) lie in a compact \\(B\\) inside the convergence region, away from the axis. Apply Lemmas \\(8.8\\)–\\(8.9\\) to\n\\(F_n=f_n\\mathbf1_B\\mathbf1_{\\{a\\le t\\le\\tau_n\\}}\\). The tuple integral tends to zero. Sum finitely many tuples and then let all deletion errors tend to zero.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are fixed choices \\( K\\ge1,\\quad0<\\eta\\le\\log2,\\quad0<\\alpha\\le\\tfrac14,\\quad0<\\gamma\\le1 \\) for which every such global candidate is zero.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Proposition \\(11.1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the geometric mesh and two-stream medium \\(B\\) of Proposition \\(7\\), with any allowed mesh phase and lower endpoint \\(l\\in[0,1]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A candidate \\(g\\ge0\\) solves\n  \\[\n  D_tg=2P(B,g)-g\\nu_B\n  \\]\n  on \\(0&lt;t&lt;\\infty\\), has locally integrable gains and losses, obeys the positive-time mild formula in Proposition \\(7\\), and satisfies \\(g\\le(t+l)H\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are fixed choices\n\\[\nK\\ge1,\\quad0&lt;\\eta\\le\\log2,\\quad0&lt;\\alpha\\le\\tfrac14,\\quad0&lt;\\gamma\\le1\n\\]\nfor which every such global candidate is zero. The choices are independent of mesh phase and \\(l\\), and precede the thermal, seed, bath, and final horizon choices. Consequently every nonzero cap limit in Propositions \\(7\\)–\\(8\\) has \\(U&lt;\\infty\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose a nonzero global candidate exists. Lemma \\(9.8\\) gives \\(t_0\\ge t_{\\mathrm{br}}\\) with positive mass \\(m_0\\) in \\(\\mathcal B_{t_0}\\). Iterating Lemma \\(9.7\\) at\n\\(t_n=(1+a_{\\mathrm{br}})^nt_0\\) gives\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathcal B_{t_n}}g(t_n)\\ge3^nm_0.\n\\]\nThe cap and \\(l\\le1\\) instead give\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathcal B_{t_n}}g(t_n)\n\\le(t_n+1)\\frac{4\\pi}{3}(d\\varkappa t_n)^3\\int_EH(v)\\,dv\n\\le C(1+t_n)^4.\n\\]\nThis contradicts \\((1+a_{\\mathrm{br}})^4&lt;3\\) as \\(n\\to\\infty\\). The initial mass and starting time may depend on \\(g\\); their sizes do not matter. Parameter choices are uniform in mesh and \\(l\\). If a nonzero cap limit had \\(U=\\infty\\), it would be such a candidate, so \\(U&lt;\\infty\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Starting with any nonnegative bounded integrable portion of \\(f(a)\\), any measurable collection of bounded finite histories gives a measure dominated by \\(f(b)\\), using weights …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(11.2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(0&lt;a&lt;b&lt;\\infty\\), and let a measurable two-beam medium be\n  \\[\n  \\widetilde B(t,x)=\\sum_{\\sigma=\\pm1}b_\\sigma(t,x)\\delta_{w_\\sigma(t,x)}.\n  \\]\n  Put \\(q_\\sigma=2\\pi b_\\sigma|v-w_\\sigma|\\), \\(\\nu=\\sum_\\sigma q_\\sigma\\), and assume chosen Borel rate representatives are bounded on compact positive-time phase-space sets.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(f\\ge0\\) solve \\(D_tf=2P(\\widetilde B,f)-\\nu f\\), with both collision terms locally integrable.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\Phi(v,w,n)=\\frac{v+w}{2}+\\frac{|v-w|}{2}n,\\qquad\n  d\\varsigma(n)=dn/(4\\pi).\n  \\]\n  A history uses ordered collision times, partner signs, directions, free flights, and updates\n  \\(V_{\\tau_i}=\\Phi(V_{\\tau_i-},w_{\\sigma_i}(\\tau_i,X_{\\tau_i}),n_i)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Starting with any nonnegative bounded integrable portion of \\(f(a)\\), any measurable collection of bounded finite histories gives a measure dominated by \\(f(b)\\), using weights\n\\[\n\\exp\\left[-\\int_a^b\\nu(s,X_s,V_s)\\,ds\\right]\n\\prod_{i=1}^j2q_{\\sigma_i}(\\tau_i,X_{\\tau_i},V_{\\tau_i-})\n\\]\nrelative to \\(\\prod_i d\\tau_i\\,d\\varsigma(n_i)\\). Empty products equal one.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma \\(1.1\\) gives collision rate \\(2\\pi|v-w|\\) and the child law \\(\\Phi_*\\varsigma\\). Both child marginals agree, and\n\\[\n\\int P(B,f)(v)\\,dv=\\int\\nu_B(v)f(v)\\,dv.\n\\]\nThus the gain coefficient two counts both children, while loss removes one parent.</p><p>In free coordinates, \\(f^\\sharp\\) has absolutely continuous sections on bounded tubes. Rate bounds make \\(\\nu^\\sharp\\) integrable on bounded positive-time flights. The scalar integrating-factor formula consists of the initial survivor plus nonnegative gain. Repeated substitution gives precisely the history weights; every remainder is nonnegative, and restriction preserves lower bounds. Tonelli justifies substitution. On bounded histories with gain rate at most \\(C\\), the \\(j\\)-term is bounded by \\(C^j(b-a)^j/j!\\) times initial mass.</p><p>The measure-input plane formula gives density for gain against point partners. Free flight and gain preserve absolute continuity from a density, so null spatial coefficient sets do not affect integrated formulas. For the actual medium,\n\\[\nb_\\sigma\\le\\theta K/(t\\alpha\\gamma),\\qquad\n\\nu_B\\le C(1+|v|)/t.\n\\]\nChoose Borel interface values from the outer annulus and zero on the axis. These bounds give positive survival on bounded finite positive-time histories and local traces. The cap passes to those traces.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(v\\in E\\), \\(p(v)\\ge25/26\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(11.3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(D=5\\), \\(E=\\{|v|\\le D\\}\\), reference medium\n  \\(B_{\\mathrm{ref}}=\\delta_{e_3}+\\delta_{-e_3}\\), and\n  \\[\n  \\nu_0(v)=2\\pi(|v-e_3|+|v+e_3|).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(J(v,dv')\\) choose sign with probabilities proportional to \\(|v-\\sigma e_3|\\), then choose the child by \\(\\Phi\\). Put \\(p(v)=J(v,E)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(v\\in E\\), \\(p(v)\\ge25/26\\). Reference branching histories retained in \\(E\\) equal a Markov flight with jump rate\n\\(\\lambda(v)=2p(v)\\nu_0(v)\\), jump law \\(J(v,dv')\\mathbf1_E(v')/p(v)\\), and weight\n\\[\n\\exp\\left(\\int_0^A\\mathcal W(V_s)\\,ds\\right),\n\\qquad \\mathcal W=(2p-1)\\nu_0.\n\\]\nOne may take\n\\[\n\\mathcal W\\ge c_0=48\\pi/13&gt;0,\\qquad \\lambda\\le C_0=48\\pi.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Set \\(s=|v|^2\\), \\(z=v_3\\), \\(R=\\sqrt{(s+1)^2-4z^2}\\). For either sign,\n\\[\n|v'|^2=(s+1+RZ)/2,\\qquad Z\\ \\text{uniform on }[-1,1].\n\\]\nThe maximum is at most \\(s+1\\), so no exit occurs for \\(s\\le24\\). For \\(24&lt;s\\le25\\), exit probability, if positive, is\n\\[\n\\frac12+\\frac{s-49}{2R}\n\\le\\frac12+\\frac{s-49}{2(s+1)}\n=\\frac{s-24}{s+1}\\le\\frac1{26}.\n\\]\nIf \\(R=0\\), speed is deterministic and stays in \\(E\\).</p><p>The Markov survival \\(e^{-\\int\\lambda}\\), multiplied by\n\\(e^{\\int(\\lambda-\\nu_0)}\\), becomes the branching survival, with identical rate-times-law factors. Bounded rates give absolute convergence of finite-time history sums. Finally \\(4\\pi\\le\\nu_0\\le24\\pi\\) on \\(E\\) and \\(2p-1\\ge12/13\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are \\(r_0,\\epsilon_0>0\\) such that, for every Borel \\(A\\subset E\\), \\( P_1(v,A)\\ge\\epsilon_0|A\\cap B(0,r_0)|,\\qquad v\\in E. \\) For every \\(\\zeta>0\\), there is \\(\\epsilon_\\zeta>0\\) such that …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(11.4\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P_t(v,\\cdot)\\) be the reference Markov velocity transition law in Lemma \\(9.2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(r_0,\\epsilon_0&gt;0\\) such that, for every Borel \\(A\\subset E\\),\n\\[\nP_1(v,A)\\ge\\epsilon_0|A\\cap B(0,r_0)|,\\qquad v\\in E.\n\\]\nFor every \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\), there is \\(\\epsilon_\\zeta&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\nP_1(v,B(e_1,\\zeta)\\cap\\{|v'|&lt;D\\})\\ge\\epsilon_\\zeta\n\\]\nuniformly in \\(v\\in E\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a sign with \\(|v-\\sigma e_3|\\ge1\\). First collide with that partner and select output within small fixed distance \\(\\delta\\) of \\(\\sigma e_3\\). Its angular probability has a uniform positive lower bound since incident distances lie in \\([1,6]\\); outputs are strictly in \\(E\\).</p><p>Next use partner \\(-\\sigma e_3\\). At exact first output \\(\\sigma e_3\\), this is the unit sphere; retain second output \\(u\\) near \\(e_1\\). Third use partner \\(e_3\\), with output\n\\[\nT(u,n)=(u+e_3)/2+|u-e_3|n/2.\n\\]\nAt \\(u=e_1\\), \\(n_0=-(e_1+e_3)/\\sqrt2\\), output is zero. Two \\(n\\)-derivatives span \\((e_1+e_3)^\\perp\\), while the tangent \\(e_3\\)-derivative in \\(u\\) is\n\\[\n\\partial_{e_3}T(e_1,n_0)=(e_1+3e_3)/4,\n\\quad (e_1+e_3)\\cdot\\partial_{e_3}T=1.\n\\]\nThus four angular coordinates map with rank three.</p><p>Use the two \\(n\\)-coordinates and one \\(u\\)-coordinate as distinguished variables. The inverse function theorem, with the remaining coordinate and first output as parameters, gives bounded Jacobians and a common image ball \\(B(0,r_0)\\), uniformly for nearby first outputs and both signs. Sphere coordinate densities are positive. Change variables and integrate the fourth coordinate to obtain a fixed positive multiple of volume.</p><p>Force three times into disjoint subintervals of \\((0,1)\\) and prohibit other jumps. Selected transition intensities\n\\(4\\pi|v-\\sigma e_3|\\mathbf1_E(\\Phi)\\,d\\varsigma\\) have positive lower bounds on the patches; survival is at least \\(e^{-C_0}\\). This proves minorization. Keeping only the first two collisions and a patch near \\(e_1\\) proves the second assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a stationary probability \\(\\pi\\), with \\(\\int v\\,d\\pi=0\\), and \\(C,c>0\\) such that \\( \\sup_{v\\in E}\\|P_t(v,\\cdot)-\\pi\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\\le Ce^{-ct}, \\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(11.5\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the reference process of Lemmas \\(9.2\\)–\\(9.3\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a stationary probability \\(\\pi\\), with \\(\\int v\\,d\\pi=0\\), and \\(C,c&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\sup_{v\\in E}\\|P_t(v,\\cdot)-\\pi\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\\le Ce^{-ct},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sup_{v\\in E}\\mathbb E_v\n\\left|A^{-1}\\int_0^AV_s\\,ds\\right|^2\\le C/A,\\qquad A\\ge1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The minorization gives a common probability portion of mass \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\) in every row of \\(P_1\\). Removing it makes \\(m\\mapsto mP_1\\) a contraction by \\(1-\\epsilon\\) in total variation. Completeness gives a unique fixed point and uniform geometric convergence. Commutation with \\(P_t\\) makes that fixed point stationary for every time; Markov contractivity gives intermediate-time estimates.</p><p>Velocity negation interchanges signs and preserves \\(E\\), so uniqueness makes \\(\\pi\\) symmetric. Its mean is zero, and\n\\(\\sup_v|\\mathbb E_vV_t|\\le Ce^{-ct}\\). The Markov property and bounded speed give\n\\[\n|\\mathbb E(V_s\\cdot V_t)|\\le Ce^{-c(t-s)},\\qquad s\\le t.\n\\]\nIntegrating on \\([0,A]^2\\) bounds the second moment of \\(\\int_0^AV_s\\,ds\\) by \\(CA\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is finite \\(L\\) such that, for every \\( A\\in I_L=[L/(1+d),L/(1-d)],\\qquad v\\in E, \\) reference branching histories staying in \\(E\\), ending strictly inside \\(E\\), and having displacement \\(Z_A\\) with …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(11.6\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(d=1/10\\) and\n  \\[\n  0&lt;\\varkappa&lt;\\min\\{1/4,c_0/(2C_0),r_0/4\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is finite \\(L\\) such that, for every\n\\[\nA\\in I_L=[L/(1+d),L/(1-d)],\\qquad v\\in E,\n\\]\nreference branching histories staying in \\(E\\), ending strictly inside \\(E\\), and having displacement \\(Z_A\\) with\n\\[\n|Z_A-\\varkappa Ae_1|&lt;\\tfrac34d\\varkappa A\n\\]\nhave total weight greater than eight.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(A_0=(1-\\varkappa)A-1\\). On the first interval of length \\(A_0\\), require integrated velocity norm at most \\(Ad\\varkappa/4\\). Lemma \\(9.4\\) and Chebyshev give probability at least \\(1/2\\) for large \\(A\\). During the next unit interval require final velocity in \\(B(e_1,d/4)\\), with uniform conditional probability \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\) by Lemma \\(9.3\\). Prohibit jumps during the final interval of length \\(\\varkappa A\\), at cost at most \\(e^{-C_0\\varkappa A}\\).</p><p>Displacement errors are at most \\(Ad\\varkappa/4,D,Ad\\varkappa/4\\), whose sum is below \\(3Ad\\varkappa/4\\) when \\(A&gt;4D/(d\\varkappa)\\). The final velocity is strictly interior. Lemma \\(9.2\\) gives branching weight at least\n\\[\n(\\epsilon/2)e^{(c_0-C_0\\varkappa)A}.\n\\]\nIts exponent is positive. Choose \\(L\\) large enough for the weight and all size conditions throughout \\(I_L\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.6", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are \\(J_{\\mathrm{br}}\\in\\mathbb N\\), \\(\\epsilon_*\\in(0,1/4)\\) such that, from every starting \\(v\\in E\\), histories with one to \\(J_{\\mathrm{br}}\\) collisions, speed at most \\(D\\), all postcollision and final …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(11.7\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use fixed \\(\\varkappa,L\\) from Lemma \\(9.5\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A measurable two-beam medium satisfies, on every speed-\\(D\\) excursion from a specified start over duration \\(A\\in I_L\\),\n  \\[\n  |b_\\sigma-1|\\le\\epsilon_*,\\qquad\n  |w_\\sigma-\\sigma e_3|\\le\\epsilon_*,\\qquad\\sigma=\\pm1.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(J_{\\mathrm{br}}\\in\\mathbb N\\), \\(\\epsilon_*\\in(0,1/4)\\) such that, from every starting \\(v\\in E\\), histories with one to \\(J_{\\mathrm{br}}\\) collisions, speed at most \\(D\\), all postcollision and final velocities strictly inside \\(E\\), and\n\\[\n|Z_A-\\varkappa Ae_1|&lt;d\\varkappa A\n\\]\nhave total weight greater than three. Initial free-flight speed may equal \\(D\\). No continuity of the medium is required.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a fixed reference start and duration, discard the zero-collision term, whose weight is at most one, leaving weight greater than seven. Monotone convergence restricts to finitely many positive counts with weight still greater than seven. Postcollision sphere measures give zero mass to \\(\\partial E\\), and degenerate collisions have zero rate. Time coincidences and endpoints may be avoided. Inner regularity in fractional time and sphere-chart coordinates restricts to finitely many compact parameter sets of total weight greater than six, with strict time, speed, and displacement margins.</p><p>Reference paths and weights vary continuously in parameters and start-duration. Each test retains weight greater than five and positive margins nearby. A finite subcover of compact \\(E\\times I_L\\) gives a uniform maximum count and margins.</p><p>Couple perturbed histories with the same directions, signs, and fractional times. Since\n\\[\n|\\Phi(v,w,n)-\\Phi(\\widetilde v,\\widetilde w,n)|\n\\le|v-\\widetilde v|+|w-\\widetilde w|,\n\\]\nvelocity errors are at most \\(j\\epsilon_*\\), and displacement errors at most\n\\(AJ_{\\mathrm{br}}\\epsilon_*\\). Medium values are compared at actual positions to constant reference partners, not to nearby medium positions. Small \\(\\epsilon_*\\) preserves margins; induction excludes a first failure of the speed restriction.</p><p>Relative-speed and full loss-rate errors are bounded by\n\\(CJ_{\\mathrm{br}}\\epsilon_*\\), and density errors give the same control for gain factors. On bounded duration intervals, survival and finite product errors are uniformly \\(O(\\epsilon_*)\\). The compact tests have finite measure, so decrease \\(\\epsilon_*\\) until their weights exceed three.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.7", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "These choices can be made independently of mesh phase and \\(l\\in[0,1]\\), with \\( (1+a_{\\mathrm{br}})^4<3. \\) There is a mesh-independent \\(t_{\\mathrm{br}}>0\\) such that every candidate in Proposition \\(9\\), on an …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(11.8\\), with the parameter choices of Section \\(11.5\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Reserve \\(\\alpha_{\\mathrm{acc}}&gt;0\\), depending only on \\(\\varkappa\\) and Lemma \\(9.3\\), so that after a first output sufficiently close to \\(\\sigma e_3\\), two further collisions with partners within \\(\\alpha_{\\mathrm{acc}}\\) of \\(-\\sigma e_3,e_3\\) have positive angular probability of ending in \\(B(\\varkappa e_1,d\\varkappa/4)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define moving boxes\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal B_t=\\{(x,v):|x-\\varkappa te_1|\\le d\\varkappa t,\\ |v|\\le D\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(\\eta&gt;0\\) small, then\n  \\(0&lt;\\alpha&lt;\\min(1/4,\\epsilon_*,\\alpha_{\\mathrm{acc}})\\), then \\(\\gamma&gt;0\\) small, and finally \\(K\\) large, as in the proof. Put\n  \\[\n  a_{\\mathrm{br}}=\\frac{L\\varkappa}{\\theta K},\\qquad\\theta=\\tfrac14.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>These choices can be made independently of mesh phase and \\(l\\in[0,1]\\), with\n\\[\n(1+a_{\\mathrm{br}})^4&lt;3.\n\\]\nThere is a mesh-independent \\(t_{\\mathrm{br}}&gt;0\\) such that every candidate in Proposition \\(9\\), on an interval containing \\([t,(1+a_{\\mathrm{br}})t]\\), \\(t\\ge t_{\\mathrm{br}}\\), satisfies\n\\[\nM((1+a_{\\mathrm{br}})t)\\ge3M(t),\\qquad\nM(s)=\\int_{\\mathcal B_s}g(s,x,v)\\,dx\\,dv.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The reserved access tolerance follows from the inverse-coordinate construction of Lemma \\(9.3\\): retain a compact positive-measure angular subset mapping into\n\\(B(\\varkappa e_1,d\\varkappa/8)\\subset B(0,r_0)\\). Uniform continuity preserves the larger target ball under sufficiently small first-output and partner perturbations, for either sign. Make \\(\\alpha_{\\mathrm{acc}}\\) smaller than half the first-output tolerance.</p><p>At \\(x_0=(y_0,z_0)\\in\\mathcal B_t\\),\n\\[\n(1-d)\\varkappa t\\le|y_0|\\le(1+d)\\varkappa t.\n\\]\nSet \\(\\Delta=L\\varkappa t/(\\theta K)\\) and\n\\(\\delta_K=DL/(\\theta K(1-d))\\). Every speed-\\(D\\) excursion over \\([t,t+\\Delta]\\) obeys\n\\[\n\\big||y|-|y_0|\\big|\\le D\\Delta,\\qquad |z|\\le d\\varkappa t+D\\Delta.\n\\]\nTake \\(K\\) so that \\(\\delta_K,a_{\\mathrm{br}}\\le1/4\\). For sufficiently large \\(t\\), these excursions avoid the lower hole, uniformly for \\(l\\le1\\). Their annular scales satisfy\n\\(e^{-\\eta}|y|\\le\\varrho\\le|y|\\), giving fixed-constant estimates\n\\[\n\\left||y_0|/\\varrho-1\\right|\\le C_1(\\eta+\\delta_K),\\quad\n\\tau/\\varrho\\le C_1/\\varkappa,\\quad\n|z-\\tau u_\\sigma|/\\varrho\\le C_1/\\varkappa.\n\\]</p><p>At each start scale time and space by \\(\\rho_0=\\theta K/|y_0|\\).\nThe duration becomes\n\\[\nA=\\rho_0\\Delta=L\\varkappa t/|y_0|\\in I_L,\n\\]\nand, with \\(S_\\sigma=S(\\gamma(z-\\tau u_\\sigma)/\\varrho)\\),\n\\[\nb_\\sigma/\\rho_0\n=\\frac{|y_0|}{\\varrho}\\frac{S_\\sigma}{1+(\\tau\\alpha\\gamma/\\varrho)S_\\sigma}.\n\\]\nSince \\(1-\\operatorname{sech}^2h\\le h^2\\),\n\\[\n|b_\\sigma/\\rho_0-1|\n\\le C_2\\bigl(\\eta+\\delta_K+(\\gamma/\\varkappa)^2+\\alpha\\gamma/\\varkappa\\bigr),\n\\qquad |u_\\sigma-\\sigma|\\le\\alpha.\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\eta,\\alpha,\\gamma\\) in the stated order so the non-\\(\\delta_K\\) terms total less than \\(\\epsilon_*/2\\); increase \\(K\\) so its term is below \\(\\epsilon_*/2\\), the preliminary bounds hold, and\n\\((1+a_{\\mathrm{br}})^4&lt;3\\). All constants precede later thermal, seed, and bath choices.</p><p>Choose \\(t_{\\mathrm{br}}\\) with\n\\((1-\\delta_K)(1-d)\\varkappa t_{\\mathrm{br}}&gt;1\\).\nLemma \\(9.6\\) applies to every scaled block. Its displacement condition returns to\n\\[\n|x_{\\mathrm{end}}-x_{\\mathrm{start}}-\\varkappa\\Delta e_1|\n&lt;d\\varkappa\\Delta,\n\\]\nso a start in \\(\\mathcal B_t\\) ends in \\(\\mathcal B_{t+\\Delta}\\). Velocities remain in \\(E\\). History weights are scaling-invariant: gain rates divide by \\(\\rho_0\\), time differentials multiply by \\(\\rho_0\\), and optical depth is unchanged. Thus the restricted finite-history kernel has mass greater than three from every start.</p><p>Integrate that measurable kernel against\n\\(\\min(g(t),m)\\mathbf1_{\\mathcal B_t}\\), apply Lemma \\(9.1\\), and let \\(m\\to\\infty\\). This proves the growth inequality without selecting different compact tests measurably at different starts.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.8", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\int_{\\mathcal B_t}g(t,x,v)\\,dx\\,dv>0 \\) at arbitrarily large finite times.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(11.9\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the fixed parameters of Lemma \\(9.7\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(g\\not\\equiv0\\) satisfy the equation, mild formula, and cap of Proposition \\(9\\) for all positive times.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\int_{\\mathcal B_t}g(t,x,v)\\,dx\\,dv&gt;0\n\\]\nat arbitrarily large finite times.</p>", "proof": "<p>At some \\(a_0&gt;0\\), restrict and truncate a positive portion of \\(g(a_0)\\) so that\n\\[\n|x|\\le R,\\quad|v|\\le R,\\quad|\\bar v|\\ge\\delta&gt;0.\n\\]\nThe axis is null, so such a positive portion exists. A long finite free flight \\(s\\) gives\n\\[\n|y+s\\bar v|\\ge s\\delta-R&gt;l+2.\n\\]\nSurvival is bounded below positively by \\(\\nu_B\\le C(1+|v|)/t\\), yielding positive bounded integrable mass outside the hole at \\(a_1=a_0+s\\).</p><p>Choose a short \\(h&gt;0\\) so paths with initial speed at most \\(R\\) and later speeds at most three remain in a bounded region \\(|y|&gt;l+1\\). Both beam densities have strictly positive lower bounds there: time, axial coordinate, and transverse radius are bounded, the radius is bounded below, annular scales are comparable to radius, \\(S\\) is positive, and the denominator is bounded above.</p><p>Force three collisions in disjoint subintervals of \\([a_1,a_1+h]\\), with no others. Choose first sign with \\(|v-\\sigma e_3|\\ge1\\), so actual relative speed is at least \\(3/4\\). Select first output close to the actual partner, hence within the reserved neighborhood of \\(\\sigma e_3\\). Next use signs \\(-\\sigma,+1\\) and the compact angular tests defining \\(\\alpha_{\\mathrm{acc}}\\). Final velocity lies in \\(B(\\varkappa e_1,d\\varkappa/4)\\); later relative speeds have positive lower bounds and postcollision speeds are at most three. Gain, angular, time, and survival factors give positive weight, even for measurable partners.</p><p>At \\(a_2=a_1+h\\), truncate to a positive bounded portion with bounded spatial support and\n\\(|v-\\varkappa e_1|&lt;d\\varkappa/4\\). If \\(R_2\\) bounds\n\\(|x-\\varkappa a_2e_1|\\), then for sufficiently large finite \\(t\\),\n\\[\n|x+(t-a_2)v-\\varkappa te_1|\n\\le R_2+(d\\varkappa/4)(t-a_2)&lt;d\\varkappa t.\n\\]\nIts speed is below \\(D\\), and finite-time survival is strictly positive. The zero-collision term of Lemma \\(9.1\\) proves the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\frac{F^d(Tt,Tx,v)}{Tm_T}\\longrightarrow0\\qquad(T\\downarrow0) \\) in local \\(L^1\\) on \\( \\mathcal V=(0,1)\\times\\mathbb R_x^3\\times\\{v\\in\\mathbb R^3:\\bar v\\ne0\\}. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is paper Lemma \\(12.1\\), used here as the final proposition in the shared pool.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix all construction parameters, first choosing the jet parameters in Proposition \\(9\\), then meeting the subsequent smallness requirements in Proposition \\(5\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F^d\\) be one dormant global limit from Proposition \\(6\\), lifted periodically and centered at the origin.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(m_T=T^p\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\frac{F^d(Tt,Tx,v)}{Tm_T}\\longrightarrow0\\qquad(T\\downarrow0)\n\\]\nin local \\(L^1\\) on\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=(0,1)\\times\\mathbb R_x^3\\times\\{v\\in\\mathbb R^3:\\bar v\\ne0\\}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The common early majorants pass to \\(F^d\\) by nonnegative testing. On a compact set with\n\\[\n0&lt;\\tau_0\\le t\\le A&lt;1,\\qquad |x|+|v|\\le R,\\qquad|\\bar v|\\ge\\epsilon&gt;0,\n\\]\nthe dormant hot bound is \\(C(Tt)^MH(v)\\), for fixed \\(M&gt;p+1\\). Division by \\(T^{p+1}\\) makes it vanish.</p><p>For small \\(T\\), \\(Tx\\) lies well inside the initial hole, with \\(D_0(Tx)\\ge\\delta\\). The bath is bounded after normalization by\n\\[\nCT^{-p-1}e^{-\\beta'|v|^2}e^{-c\\delta^2/(TA)}\\to0.\n\\]</p><p>A main enlargement at transverse position \\(Ty\\) has scale \\(s\\le C_RT\\). Only the origin copy can be main: another would require a nonzero lattice center with transverse distance at most \\(s_0+o(1)\\) and axial distance at most \\(a+b_{\\mathrm{sp}}+o(1)&lt;1/2\\), contradicting unit lattice spacing. For the main color,\n\\[\nc_{\\mathrm{main}}(Tt,Tx,v)\n\\le Cs^{-31}\\exp[-c\\epsilon^2/(d_0^2s^{20})].\n\\]\nIts supremum over \\(0&lt;s\\le C_RT\\) decays faster than every power of \\(T\\). At each point there is at most one main color. Nonmain sums are bounded by\n\\(C_M(Tt)^Me^{-4|v|^2}\\) for every fixed \\(M&gt;0\\), so choosing \\(M&gt;p+1\\) handles the same normalization. All components vanish locally in \\(L^1\\).</p>"}], "edges": [[2, 1, "part"], [3, 1, "part"], [4, 1, "part"], [5, 1, "part"], [6, 1, "part"], [7, 1, "part"], [8, 1, "part"], [10, 9, "part"], [11, 9, "part"], [12, 9, "part"], [14, 13, "part"], [15, 13, "part"], [16, 13, "part"], [17, 13, "part"], [18, 13, "part"], [19, 13, "part"], [21, 20, "part"], [22, 20, "part"], [23, 20, "part"], [24, 20, "part"], [25, 20, "part"], [27, 26, "part"], [28, 26, "part"], [29, 26, "part"], [31, 30, "part"], [34, 33, "part"], [35, 33, "part"], [36, 33, "part"], [37, 33, "part"], [38, 33, "part"], [39, 33, "part"], [40, 33, "part"], [41, 33, "part"], [42, 33, "part"], [43, 33, "part"], [45, 44, "part"], [46, 44, "part"], [47, 44, "part"], [48, 44, "part"], [49, 44, "part"], [50, 44, "part"], [51, 44, "part"], [52, 44, "part"]]});
